{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 77 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2f05a6d6a5ce48af309f543e0888446738d5fc412b046e17fedc6a4a26e98de4" :edb/db-before-t 76 :edb/db-after-t 77 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533389 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:43:54.775Z" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186054578 1071 "Given $xy+y^2=x^2$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186054578 1071 "Given $xy+y^2=x^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186054578 1074 "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$, assuming that $y = y(x)$.\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy + y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) + \\frac{d}{dx}(y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\\\\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y\\frac{d}{dx}(x) + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2x \\\\\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2x \\\\\n(x + 2y)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2x - y \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{2x - y}{x + 2y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186054578 1074 "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ (x+2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186054579 1071 "Let $f(x) = 3 x^4 - 5 x^3 - 3 x^2 + 6 x - 8$. If $x=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ are zeros of $f(x)$, factor $f(x)$ completely." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186054579 1071 "Let $f(x) = 3 x^4 - 5 x^3 - 3 x^2 + 6 x - 8.$ If $x=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ are zeros of $f(x),$ factor $f(x)$ completely." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186054579 1074 "Because $x=2$ and\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nare both zeros of $f(x)$, the factor theorem guarantees that $(x-2)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nare both factors of $f(x)$. So, $(x-2)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nboth divide $f(x)$.\n\nTo begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by one of these factors (it doesn't matter which one we divide first). Let's divide $f(x)$ by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $6$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 3x^{4} - 5x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 6x - 8 \\\\\n&= (x - 2)(3x^{3} + x^{2} - x + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, because\n\n$$\n\\left(x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nis also a factor of $f(x)$, it must be a factor of the quotient $3x^3 + x^2 - x +4$. So, we divide this quotient by\n\n$$\n\\left(x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nusing synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{3} + x^{2} - x + 4 &= (x + \\frac{4}{3})(3x^{2} - 3x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x + \\frac{4}{3})(3)(x^{2} - x + 1) \\\\\n&= (3x + 4)(x^{2} - x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{4} - 5x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 6x - 8 &= (x - 2)(3x + 4)(x^{2} - x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quadratic polynomial $x^2-x+1$ does not appear to factor over the real numbers. We can make sure of this by verifying that its discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\nD = b^{2} - 4ac = (-1)^{2} - 4(1)(1) =-3 < 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-2)(3x+4)(x^2-x+1)\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186054579 1074 "Because $x=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ are both zeros of $f(x),$ the factor theorem guarantees that $(x-2)$ and $\\left( x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ are both factors of $f(x).$ So, $(x-2)$ and $\\left( x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ both divide $f(x).$\n\nTo begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by one of these factors (it doesn't matter which one we divide first). Let's divide $f(x)$ by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $6$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-8 \\\\  & =(x-2)(3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, because $\\left( x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $3x^3 + x^2 - x +4.$ So, we divide this quotient by $\\left( x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x+4 & =(x+\\frac{4}{3})(3{x}^{2}-3x+3) \\\\  & =(x+\\frac{4}{3})(3)({x}^{2}-x+1) \\\\  & =(3x+4)({x}^{2}-x+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-8 & =(x-2)(3x+4)({x}^{2}-x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quadratic polynomial $x^2-x+1$ does not appear to factor over the real numbers. We can make sure of this by verifying that its discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={b}^{2}-4ac=(-1)^{2}-4(1)(1)=-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-2)(3x+4)(x^2-x+1).\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186054580 1071 "Given that $(x-1)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of the polynomial $f(x)= x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6$, factor $f(x)$ completely." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186054580 1071 "Given that $(x-1)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of the polynomial $f(x)= x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6,$ factor $f(x)$ completely." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186054580 1074 "To factor $f(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6}{x-1} = x^3-7x-6\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x^3-7x-6)\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x)$, it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^3-7x-6)$. So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-7x-6}{x-3} = x^2+3x +2\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x-3) (x^2+3x +2)\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x-3) (x+2)(x+1)\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186054580 1074 "To factor $f(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6}{x-1} = x^3-7x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x^3-7x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^3-7x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-7x-6}{x-3} = x^2+3x +2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x^2+3x +2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x+2)(x+1).\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186054581 1071 "Let $p(x) = x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4$. Knowing that $p(4) = p(-1) = 0$, factor the polynomial $p(x)$ completely." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186054581 1071 "Let $p(x) = x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4.$ Knowing that $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ factor the polynomial $p(x)$ completely." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186054581 1074 "Since\n\n$$\np(4) = p(-1) = 0\n$$\n\nthe factor theorem guarantees that $(x - 4)$ and $(x + 1)$ are both factors of $p(x)$.\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x - 4)$ is a factor of $p(x)$, we know that $(x - 4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x)$, we can divide it by $(x - 4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-9$ | $-11$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $12$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4}{x-4} = x^3+3x^2+3x+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4) (x^3+3x^2+3x+1)\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $p(x)$, it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^3+3x^2+3x+1)$. So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3+3x^2+3x-1}{x+1} = x^2+2x+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4)(x+1)(x^2+2x+1)\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{4} - x^{3} - 9x^{2} - 11x - 4 &= (x - 4)(x + 1)(x + 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= (x - 4)(x + 1)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186054581 1074 "Since $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ the factor theorem guarantees that $(x - 4)$ and $(x + 1)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x - 4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x - 4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-9$ | $-11$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $12$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4}{x-4} = x^3+3x^2+3x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4) (  x^3+3x^2+3x+1).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $( x^3+3x^2+3x+1).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ x^3+3x^2+3x-1}{x+1} = x^2+2x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4)(x+1)(x^2+2x+1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}-11x-4 & =(x-4)(x+1)(x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186059612 1071 "If $z_1=3\\textrm{i}$ and $z_2 = 2+5\\textrm{i}$, then what is $z_1$ divided by $z_2$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186059612 1071 "If $z_1=3\\textrm{i}$ and $z_2 = 2+5\\textrm{i},$ then what is $z_1$ divided by $z_2?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186059612 1074 "First, we set up the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\textrm{i}}{2+5\\textrm i}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is $2+5\\textrm{i}$ and its conjugate is $2-5\\textrm{i}$. We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $2-5\\textrm{i}$, so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {3 \\textrm{i}} {2 +5\\textrm{i}} &= \\dfrac {3 \\textrm{i}} {2 +5 \\textrm{i}} \\cdot \\dfrac {2 -5 \\textrm{i}} {2-5\\textrm{i}} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} - 15 \\textrm{i}^2} {2^2 - (5\\textrm{i})^2} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} - 15\\textrm{i}^2} {4 - 25\\textrm{i}^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} - 15(-1)} {4 - 25(-1)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} + 15} {29} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b \\textrm{i}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} + 15} {29}= \\dfrac{15} {29} + \\dfrac {6} {29} \\textrm{i} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186059612 1074 "First, we set up the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\textrm{i}}{2+5\\textrm i}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is $2+5\\textrm{i}$ and its conjugate is $2-5\\textrm{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $2-5\\textrm{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {3 \\textrm{i}} {2 +5\\textrm{i}} \n&= \\dfrac {3 \\textrm{i}} {2 +5 \\textrm{i}} \\cdot \\dfrac {2 -5 \\textrm{i}} {2-5\\textrm{i}}  \\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} - 15 \\textrm{i}^2 } {2^2 - (5\\textrm{i})^2}  \\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} - 15\\textrm{i}^2 } {4 - 25\\textrm{i}^2} \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} - 15(-1) } {4 - 25(-1)}  \\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} + 15 } {29}\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b \\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {6 \\textrm{i} + 15 } {29}=\n\t\t\\dfrac{15} {29} + \\dfrac {6} {29} \\textrm{i}\t\n\t}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186059613 1071 "Express $\\dfrac{4}{-1+2\\textrm{i} }$ in the form $a+\\textrm{i}b.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186059613 1071 "Express $\\dfrac{4}{-1+2\\textrm{i}}$ in the form $a+\\textrm{i}b$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186059613 1074 "The denominator is $-1+2\\textrm{i}$ and its conjugate is $-1-2\\textrm{i}$. We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-1-2\\textrm{i}$, 