he first and second quadrants.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) =\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186059615 1074 "The range of $\\theta = \\arccos x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0 \\le \\theta  \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(  -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) =\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186059616 1071 "Find, in radians, the value of $\\arctan(-\\sqrt 3)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186059616 1071 "Find, in radians, the value of $\\arctan(-\\sqrt 3).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186059616 1074 "First, note that the range of\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt 3$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt 3\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $\\arctan$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(-\\sqrt 3\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186059616 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arctan x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt 3$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt 3.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $\\arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(-\\sqrt 3\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186059626 1071 "Find the value of $\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2 \\right)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186059626 1071 "Find the value of $\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2 \\right).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186059626 1074 "First, note that the range of\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arcsin x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\le \\theta \\le \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\n$$\n\nHowever,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\ndoes not lie within the range of $\\theta$. To get an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nand lies within the range of $\\theta$, we subtract $2\\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186059626 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arcsin x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\le \\theta \\le \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left( \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta.$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ and lies within the range of $\\theta,$ we subtract $2\\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064118 1071 "If $f(x)=2x+\\left\\lfloor \\dfrac{x}{5} \\right\\rfloor$, then what is the value of $f(-0.5)$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064118 1071 "If $f(x)=2x+\\left\\lfloor \\dfrac{x}{5} \\right\\rfloor,$ then what is the value of $f(-0.5)?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064118 1074 "Suppose $x$ is a real number. The greatest integer (or floor) function $\\lfloor x \\rfloor$ equals the greatest integer that is smaller than or equal to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lfloor x \\rfloor = n,\n\\quad\\text{where}\\quad\nn \\leq x \\lt n+1,\\quad  n\\in \\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x=-0.5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-0.5) & =2\\cdot (-0.5)+⌊\\frac{-0.5}{5}⌋ \\\\  & =-1+⌊-0.1⌋.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the greatest integer function we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⌊-0.1⌋=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-0.5) & =-1+⌊-0.1⌋ \\\\  & =-1+(-1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064118 1074 "Suppose $x$ is a real number. The greatest integer (or floor) function $\\lfloor x \\rfloor$ equals the greatest integer that is smaller than or equal to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lfloor x \\rfloor = n, \\quad\\text{where}\\quad n \\leq x \\lt n+1,\\quad n\\in \\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x=-0.5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-0.5) &= 2 \\cdot (-0.5) + ⌊\\frac{-0.5}{5}⌋ \\\\\n&=-1 + ⌊ - 0.1⌋\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the greatest integer function we have\n\n$$\n⌊ - 0.1⌋ =-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-0.5) &= -1 + ⌊ - 0.1⌋ \\\\\n&=-1 + (-1) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064119 1071 "Find the value of $f(x)=(\\lceil 6x-3 \\rceil -2)^3$ at $x=0.4$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064119 1071 "Find the value of $f(x)=(\\lceil 6x-3 \\rceil -2)^3$ at $x=0.4.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064119 1074 "Suppose that $x$ is a real number. The least integer (or ceiling) function $\\lceil x \\rceil$ equals the smallest integer that is greater than or equal to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lceil x \\rceil = n, \\quad\\text{where}\\quad n-1 \\lt x \\leq n, \\quad n\\in\\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x=0.4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0.4) &= (⌈6(0.4) - 3⌉ - 2)^{3} \\\\\n&= (⌈2.4 - 3⌉ - 2)^{3} \\\\\n&= (⌈ - 0.6⌉ - 2)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the least integer function, we have\n\n$$\n\\lceil -0.6 \\rceil = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0.4) &= (⌈ - 0.6⌉ - 2)^{3} \\\\\n&= (0 - 2)^{3} \\\\\n&= (-2)^{3} \\\\\n&=-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064119 1074 "Suppose that $x$ is a real number. The least integer (or ceiling) function $\\lceil x \\rceil$ equals the smallest integer that is greater than or equal to $x{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\lceil x \\rceil = n,\n\\quad\\text{where}\\quad\nn-1 \\lt x \\leq n, \\quad n\\in\\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x=0.4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.4) & =(⌈6(0.4)-3⌉-2)^{3} \\\\  & =(⌈2.4-3⌉-2)^{3} \\\\  & =(⌈-0.6⌉-2)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the least integer function, we have\n\n$$\n\\lceil -0.6 \\rceil = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.4) & =(⌈-0.6⌉-2)^{3} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064120 1071 "A rocket is at a distance of $r(t)$ meters from the ground $t$ seconds after it is launched. What are the units of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064120 1071 "A rocket is at a distance of $r(t)$ meters from the ground $t$ seconds after it is launched. What are the units of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064120 1074 "We have that the height $r(t)$ is measured in meters and the time is measured in seconds.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}r(t): & meters \\\\ t: & seconds\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nmeasures a change in $r(t)$ over a change in $t$. So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dr}{dt}: \\mid \\frac{meters}{seconds}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nis measured in \"meters per second\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064120 1074 "We have that the height $r(t)$ is measured in meters and the time is measured in seconds. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t): & \\text{meters} \\\\ t: & \\text{seconds}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$ measures a change in $r(t)$ over a change in $t.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{meters}}{\\text{seconds}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$ is measured in \"meters per second\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064121 1071 "Howard, the manager of a sci-fi convention, uses the following three functions to model the daily number of visitors to the convention, $t$ minutes after the convention has opened.\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of people inside the convention\n\n- $E(t)$ - the rate at which people enter the convention\n\n- $L(t)$ - the rate at which people leave the convention\n\nWhat is the relationship between the three functions above?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064121 1071 "Howard, the manager of a sci-fi convention, uses the following three functions to model the daily number of visitors to the convention, $t$ minutes after the convention has opened.\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of people inside the convention\n- $E(t)$ - the rate at which people enter the convention\n- $L(t)$ - the rate at which people leave the convention\n\nWhat is the relationship between the three functions above?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064121 1074 "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of people inside the convention $t$ minutes after opening. Then,\n\n$$\nN'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}N(t)}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Change in }N}{\\textrm{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, $N'(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of people inside the convention changes $t$ minutes after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of people inside the convention at time $t$ by finding the difference between the entry and exit rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\nN'(t) = E(t) - L(t).\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064121 1074 "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of people inside the convention $t$ minutes after opening. Then,\n\n$$\nN'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}N(t)}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Change in}N}{\\textrm{Change in}t}\n$$\n\nThus, $N'(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of people inside the convention changes $t$ minutes after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of people inside the convention at time $t$ by finding the difference between the entry and exit rates at time $t$. That is,\n\n$$\nN'(t) = E(t) - L(t)\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064122 1074 "A stationary point of $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ where $f'(x)=0$. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=0$ and $x=4$, the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have $f'(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](images/56/56e798c0942ddcf0b1786afb054ab6dfbea7768c86751555808d86cc4cfa89f6.png)\n\nSo, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=0$ and $x=4$.\n\n**Note:** At $x=2$, there is a sharp corner, so $f'(2)$ doesn't exist. This gives one more critical point. But this is not a stationary point." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064122 1074 "A stationary point of $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ where $f'(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.  \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have $f'(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](images/56/56e798c0942ddcf0b1786afb054ab6dfbea7768c86751555808d86cc4cfa89f6.png)\n\n  \n\nSo, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=0$ and $x=4$.\n\n**Note:** At $x=2$, there is a sharp corner, so $f'(2)$ doesn't exist. This gives one more critical point. But this is not a stationary point." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064123 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/5} x\\sin(5x) \\, \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064123 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/5} x\\sin(5x) \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064123 1074 "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\int (-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+{\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5})|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)]+[\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{\\pi }{25}\\cdot (-1)+0]+[0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064123 1074 "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u = x & ⟹u^{′} = 1 \\\\ v^{′} = \\sin (5x) & ⟹v =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cos (5x)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intuv^{′}dx &= uv - \\intvu^{′}dx \\\\\n\\intx \\cdot \\sin (5x)dx &= -x \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\cos (5x) - \\int(-\\frac{1}{5}\\cos (5x)) \\cdot 1dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/5}x\\sin (5x)dx &= \\left.- \\frac{1}{5}x\\cos (5x)\\right|_{0}^{π/5} + \\frac{1}{5}\\int_{0}^{π/5}\\cos (5x)dx \\\\\n&= \\left.- \\frac{1}{5}x\\cos (5x)\\right|_{0}^{π/5} + \\left.\\frac{1}{5}((\\sin (5x))/(5))\\right|_{0}^{π/5} \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot \\frac{π}{5}\\cos (π) + \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 0 \\cdot \\cos (0)] + [\\frac{1}{25}\\sin (π) - \\frac{1}{25}\\sin (0)] \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{π}{25} \\cdot (-1) + 0] + [0 - 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064129 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]$?\n\n1. $f(x)= 3^{x} - 1$\n2. $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$\n3. $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064129 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)= 3^{x} - 1$\n\n- $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$\n\n- $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064129 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]$, the function must be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$.\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 3^{x} - 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty)$. In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$. Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n- The function $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty)$. In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n- The function $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x = 0$, hence, it is not continuous at\n$x = 0$, and so it is not continuous on $[0,1]$. Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064129 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 3^{x} - 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x = 0,$ hence, it is not continuous at \n$x = 0,$ and so it is not continuous on $[0,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1].$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064130 1071 "![](images/7d/7d62ee465505708dc91f486cf57953dc5058ad275fb5462b7217114d5c3e8ea0.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x)$, defined on $[-3,3]$. Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064130 1071 "![](images/7d/7d62ee465505708dc91f486cf57953dc5058ad275fb5462b7217114d5c3e8ea0.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064130 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downward like a frown ($\\color{black}{\\frown}$) over the intervals $(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3).$\n\n![](images/ad/ad6e25c0611cda21ffcd41114afc4d601779edb2f31288ab16dd31207bf83f65.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064130 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downward like a frown ($\\color{black}{\\frown}$) over the intervals $(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3)$.\n\n![](images/ad/ad6e25c0611cda21ffcd41114afc4d601779edb2f31288ab16dd31207bf83f65.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064131 1071 "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 2\\sin (3x - 1)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064131 1071 "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 2\\sin (3x - 1).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064131 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function\n\n$$\nf = 2\\sin u, \\qquad u=3x-1\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 2\\cos u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{df}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= 2\\cos u \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 6\\cos u \\\\\n&= 6\\cos (3x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064131 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function\n\n$$\nf = 2\\sin u, \\qquad u=3x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 2\\cos u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064132 1071 "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ if $y = 4\\csc (5x + 3)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064132 1071 "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ if $y = 4\\csc (5x + 3).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064132 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 4 \\csc u, \\qquad u = 5x+3\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -4\\csc u\\cot u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&=-4\\csc u\\cot u \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&=-20\\csc u\\cot u \\\\\n&=-20\\csc (5x + 3)\\cot (5x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064132 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 4 \\csc u, \\qquad u = 5x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -4\\csc u\\cot u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x+3)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064133 1071 "Given that $f(x) = \\sin^3{x}$, calculate $f'(x)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064133 1071 "Given that $f(x) = \\sin^3{x}$, calculate $f'(x).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064133 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = (\\sin{x})^3\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = u^3, \\qquad u=\\sin x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 3u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{df}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= 3u^{2} \\cdot (\\cos x) \\\\\n&= 3(\\sin x)^{2}\\cos x \\\\\n&= 3\\sin^{2} x\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064133 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = (\\sin{x})^3.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = u^3, \\qquad u=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 3u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064135 1071 "Find the derivative of $y = \\tan{\\left(\\sin(x)\\right)}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064135 1071 "Find the derivative of $y = \\tan{\\left(\\sin(x)\\right)}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186064135 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan u, \\qquad u = \\sin x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec^2 u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\sec^{2} u \\cdot \\cos x \\\\\n&= \\sec^{2} (\\sin x)\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186064135 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan u, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec^2 u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186068625 1071 "Graph the function $y = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186068625 1071 "Graph the function $y = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186068625 1074 "To plot\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}$.\n\n![](images/9d/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\n\n- Translate it by one unit to the right to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x-1}$.![](images/8c/8ce8c53f202f04b3b34a247b797f65f7616e8772afa1751de126ddd401417bd7.png)\nNotice that the vertical asymptote is now $x=1$.\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}$.\n\n![](images/c9/c9b708a20d9c6c12f0016279e8d7a3ea55597e7645f0a9518f1c32e8a8406e47.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit down to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1$.![](images/e1/e100b552adb71cae1295492a58b0e60525813adb87d9815ba64a151a856f0c46.png)\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote is now $y=-1$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186068625 1074 "To plot $y = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/9d/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\n\n- Translate it by one unit to the right to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x-1}.$ \n\n![](images/8c/8ce8c53f202f04b3b34a247b797f65f7616e8772afa1751de126ddd401417bd7.png)\n\nNotice that the vertical asymptote is now $x=1.$\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}.$\n\n![](images/c9/c9b708a20d9c6c12f0016279e8d7a3ea55597e7645f0a9518f1c32e8a8406e47.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit down to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1.$ \n\n![](images/e1/e100b552adb71cae1295492a58b0e60525813adb87d9815ba64a151a856f0c46.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote is