 by the parametric equations $x =e^{t}, \\, y= e^{2t}+1.$ Given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= 2e^t,$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186068627 1074 "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dt}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= 2e^t\n$$\n\nand we can calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} (e^t) = e^t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(2e^{t}))/(e^{t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2e^{t}}{e^{t}} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186068627 1074 "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= 2e^t,$ and we can calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} (e^t) = e^t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2{e}^{t})}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186068628 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = \\sin(2t), \\qquad y= 2\\cos (2t) \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt \\pi$.\n\nGiven that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -2 \\tan(2t)$, calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac\\pi 8$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186068628 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx =  \