{0}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{1+3}})}{(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{0+3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{3}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}})(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{4}^{0}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186078362 1071 "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}(t) =  \\left<   9\\cos {3t},\\, \\sin{t} \\right>.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ and has velocity $\\left\\langle 2,\\,0  \\right\\rangle$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186078362 1071 "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\left< 9\\cos {3t},\\, \\sin{t} \\right>$.\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ and has velocity $\\left\\langle 2,\\,0 \\right\\rangle$ when $t=0$, calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186078362 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\inta(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int⟨9\\cos 3t, \\sin t⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨\\int9\\cos 3tdt, \\int\\sin tdt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3\\sin 3t + C_{1},-\\cos t + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} (0)= \\langle 2,\\, 0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(0) &= ⟨3\\sin 0 + C_{1},-\\cos 0 + C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨2, 0⟩ &= ⟨C_{1},-1 + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=2$ and $C_2=1$. Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\left< 3\\sin{3t} +2, \\, 1-\\cos{t} \\right>\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= \\intv(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int⟨3\\sin 3t + 2, 1 - \\cos t⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨\\int3\\sin 3t + 2dt, \\int1 - \\cos tdt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - \\cos 3t + 2t + C_{3}, t - \\sin t + C_{4}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle 0,0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨ - \\cos 0 + 2(0) + C_{3}, 0 - \\sin 0 + C_{4}⟩ \\\\\n⟨0, 0⟩ &= ⟨ - 1 + C_{3}, C_{4}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_3=1$ and $C_4=0$. Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left< -\\cos {3t} + 2t + 1, \\, t-\\sin{t} \\right>\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186078362 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{v} (0)= \\langle 2,\\, 0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,\\,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=2$ and $C_2=1.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\left<      3\\sin{3t} +2 , \\,    1-\\cos{t} \\right>.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2t+{C}_{3},\\,t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2(0)+{C}_{3},\\,0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{3},\\,{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_3=1$ and $C_4=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left<  -\\cos {3t} + 2t + 1 , \\,   t-\\sin{t}  \\right>.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186078364 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} }.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186078364 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4}}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186078364 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4^{+})\\frac{x - 2\\sqrt{x}}{x - 4} &= \\frac{4 - 2\\sqrt{4}}{4 - 4} = \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {(x -2 \\sqrt{x})(x+2\\sqrt x)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-\\left(2\\sqrt{x}\\right)^2} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-4x} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x(x-4)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x} {x+2\\sqrt x} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {4} {4+2\\sqrt4} \\\\ &=\\dfrac 1 2. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186078364 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-2\\sqrt{x}}{x-4} & =\\frac{4-2\\sqrt{4}}{4-4}=\\frac{0}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {(x -2 \\sqrt{x})(x+2\\sqrt x)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n            &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-\\left(2\\sqrt{x}\\right)^2} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x^2-4x} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x(x-4)} {(x - 4)(x+2\\sqrt x)} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x} {x+2\\sqrt x} \\\n\t    &= \\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {4} {4+2\\sqrt4} \\\n\t    &=\\dfrac 1 2.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083050 1071 "The quadratic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots $x_1=2, x_2=5$, and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $3$. Find $P(x)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083050 1071 "The quadratic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots $x_1=2, x_2=5,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $3.$ Find $P(x).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083050 1074 "Since our quadratic polynomial has roots\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}x_1=2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}x_2=5}\n$$\n\nand the coefficient of the quadratic term is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}a=3}\n$$\n\nwe can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(x - x_{1})(x - x_{2}) &= 3(x - 2)(x - 5) \\\\\n&= 3(x - 2)(x - 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(x - 2)(x - 5) &= 3(x^{2} - 7x + 10) \\\\\n&= 3x^{2} - 21x + 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(x) = 3x^2-21x+30\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083050 1074 "Since our quadratic polynomial has roots ${\\color{black}x_1=2}$ and ${\\color{black}x_2=5},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is ${\\color{black}a=3},$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}) & =3(x-2)(x-5) \\\\  & =3(x-2)(x-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x-2)(x-5) & =3({x}^{2}-7x+10) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-21x+30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x) = 3x^2-21x+30.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083060 1071 "For the parametric curve\n$x=\\cos{t}, \\quad y=\\sin{t}, \\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi$, calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$.\n\n![](images/2e/2ec95dd061fa6b7f09c9f899a1f6483b4ea7d5127372ddd6eefb74d6ed10c14c.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083060 1071 "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\cos{t}, \\quad y=\\sin{t}, \\qquad  0\\leq t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$\n\n![](images/2e/2ec95dd061fa6b7f09c9f899a1f6483b4ea7d5127372ddd6eefb74d6ed10c14c.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083060 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y$, we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -\\sin{t}, \\qquad y'(t)=\\cos{t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\cos t}{-\\sin t} = -\\cot t\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083060 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -\\sin{t}, \\qquad y'(t)=\\cos{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\cos t}{-\\sin t} = -\\cot t.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083061 1071 "For the parametric curve\n$x=te^t, \\quad y=e^{-t}, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty$,\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083061 1071 "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=te^t, \\quad y=e^{-t}, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083061 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y$, we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t + te^t = (1+t)e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=-e^{-t}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (-e^{-t})/((1 + t)e^{t}) \\\\\n&= - (1)/((1 + t)e^{2t})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1$, the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{t = 1} &= - (1)/(e^{2(1)}(1 + 1)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2e^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083061 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t + te^t = (1+t)e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=-e^{-t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-{e}^{-t}}{(1+t){e}^{t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1+t){e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2(1)}(1+1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083062 1071 "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty$.\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083062 1071 "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083062 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2(-1) + 3 = 1, \\\\\ny &= (-1)^{3} - 4(-1) =-1 + 4 = 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3)$. The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](images/b6/b6eca92a587d26a7dcb610e036c6ac4e307c082ed49072be1cc076e8e00c156b.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTo compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$, the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm = \\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{t =-1} &= (3(-1)^{2} - 4)/(2) =-\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)\n$$\n\nand the slope\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right)\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083062 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(-1)+3=1, \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}-4(-1)=-1+4=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3).$ The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](images/0f/0fa3fc12e83e2a581499680145df9229917c7426e6a3d12c51d88e115a42033e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4 .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-4}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right).\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083063 1071 "![](images/2c/2c0555e93c4674e6dd21393fe02d39bd9f249769b16745a3e16ee7df4d307ded.png)\n\nThe following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n$x=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi$\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083063 1071 "![](images/9d/9dcc4c97bd296ef4627b02b6cfafcdb643f475cd8dacd36a820db760bd6fb045.png)\n\nThe following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083063 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ninto the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2\\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) + \\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} + 1, \\\\\ny &= \\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) - 2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2(1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right)\n$$\n\nFor this, we need to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwhich means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(t))/(x^{′}(t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-\\sin t - 4\\cos 2t}{-2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t} \\\\\n&= - (\\sin t + 4\\cos 2t)/(2(\\cos 2t - \\sin t))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nthe slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{t = π/4} \\\\\n&= - (\\sin (\\frac{π}{4}) + 4\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}))/(2(\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) - \\sin (\\frac{π}{4}))) \\\\\n&= - (\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2} + 4(0))/(2(0 - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2) &= -2(x - (\\sqrt{2} + 1)) \\\\\ny - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 2 &= -2x + 2\\sqrt{2} + 2 \\\\\ny + 2x &= \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083063 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1, \\\\  \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right).$ For this, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}+4(0)}{2(0-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2,\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2) & =-2(x-(\\sqrt{2}+1)) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2 & =-2x+2\\sqrt{2}+2 \\\\ y+2x & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083071 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nA tetrahedral die has four sides. Let $X$ be the score attained on a random roll of the die. Then the support is\n\n$$\nS = \\{1,2,3,4 \\}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding probabilities are all\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\nf(1) = f(2) = f(3) = f(4) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= 1 \\cdot f(1) + 2 \\cdot f(2) + 3 \\cdot f(3) + 4 \\cdot f(4) \\\\\n&= 1(\\frac{1}{4}) + 2(\\frac{1}{4}) + 3(\\frac{1}{4}) + 4(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{2}{4} + \\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{4}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + 2 + 3 + 4}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{4} \\\\\n&= 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083071 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nA tetrahedral die has four sides. Let $X$ be the score attained on a random roll of the die. Then the support is $S = \\{ 1,2,3,4 \\},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\dfrac{1}{4}{:}$\n\n$$\nf(1) = f(2) = f(3) = f(4) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{4})+2(\\frac{1}{4})+3(\\frac{1}{4})+4(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2+3+4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{4} \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083072 1071 "Suppose that we play a game with a stack of five cards labeled $1$ through $5$. A card is drawn at random. Drawing $1,2,3$, or $4$ means that you lose $\\[math]1$, and drawing a $5$ means that you win $\\[/math] 5$. What is the expected profit at each drawing in this game?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083072 1071 "Suppose that we play a game with a stack of five cards labeled $1$ through $5.$ A card is drawn at random. Drawing $1,2,3,$ or $4$ means that you lose $\\[math]1,$ and drawing a $5$ means that you win $\\[/math] 5.$ What is the expected profit at each drawing in this game?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083072 1074 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a draw in this game. On each draw in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- drawing $1,2,3,4$, the player loses $\\[math]1$, so we have $p=-1$.\n- drawing $5$, the player wins $\\[/math]5$, so we have $p=5$.\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-1)=\\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad P(5)=\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single draw, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p$, then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $-1$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\dfrac{4}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\textrm E[p] &= -1 \\cdot f(-1) + 5 \\cdot f(5) \\\\[5pt] &= -1 \\left(\\dfrac{4}{5} \\right) + 5 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{5} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &=-\\dfrac{4}{5} + 1 \\\\[3pt] &=\\dfrac{1}{5} \\\\[3pt] &=0.2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit at each draw in this game is $\\[math]0.20$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083072 1074 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a draw in this game. On each draw in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- drawing $1,2,3,4$, the player loses $\\[math]1,$ so we have $p=-1.$\n\n- drawing $5$, the player wins $\\[/math]5,$ so we have $p=5.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-1)=\\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad P(5)=\\dfrac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single draw, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $-1$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\dfrac{4}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\textrm E[p] &= -1 \\cdot f(-1) + 5 \\cdot f(5)  \\[5pt]\n&= -1 \\left( \\dfrac{4}{5} \\right) + 5 \\left( \\dfrac{1}{5} \\right)  \\[5pt]\n\t&=-\\dfrac{4}{5} + 1 \\[3pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{1}{5} \\[3pt]\n\t&=0.2\n\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit at each draw in this game is $\\[math]0.20$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083076 1071 "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 3t^3, 2t^2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083076 1071 "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t > 0$ is given by\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 3t^3, 2t^2\\rangle$.\nCalculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083076 1074 "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{dx}{dt}, \\frac{dy}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(3t^{3}), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨9t^{2}, 4t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083076 1074 "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083077 1071 "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 12t, 2t^3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t=1.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083077 1071 "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t > 0$ is given by\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 12t, 2t^3\\rangle$.\nCalculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t=1$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083077 1074 "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{dx}{dt}, \\frac{dy}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(12t), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨12, 6t^{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=1$, we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(1) &= ⟨12, 6(1)^{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨12, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083077 1074 "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,6{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=1$, we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨12,6(1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083078 1071 "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 6t^2+2, 2t^3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=2.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083078 1071 "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t > 0$ is given by\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 6t^2+2, 2t^3\\rangle$.\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=2$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083078 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}$. Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{dx}{dt}, \\frac{dy}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(6t^{2} + 2), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨12t, 6t^{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2$, we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(2) &= ⟨12(2), 6(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨24, 24⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=2$ we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf{v}(2)$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid v(2) \\mid &= \\sqrt{24^{2} + 24^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2(24^{2})} \\\\\n&= 24\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed when $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n24\\sqrt{2}\\approx 33.94\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083078 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12t,6{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2$, we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨12(2),6(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨24,24⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=2$ we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf{v}(2),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(2)| & =\\sqrt{{24}^{2}+{24}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2({24}^{2})} \\\\  & =24\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed when $t=2$ is $24\\sqrt{2}\\approx 33.94$ to two decimal places." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083079 1071 "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle t^2-2t, t^3-3t \\rangle, \\quad t\\in[0,\\infty).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083079 1071 "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\langle t^2-2t, t^3-3t \\rangle, \\quad t\\in[0,\\infty)$.\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083079 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}$. Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{dx}{dt}, \\frac{dy}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - 2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3} - 3t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2t - 2, 3t^{2} - 3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= 0 \\\\\n⟨2t - 2, 3t^{2} - 3⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2t - 2 &= 0⇒t = 1, \\\\\n3t^{2} - 3 &= 0⇒t = ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $t=-1$ is outside of the domain of $t$, so we discard it.\n\nThe solution $t=1$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only time when the object is stationary." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083079 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,3{t}^{2}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2t-2,3{t}^{2}-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=1, \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-3 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $t=-1$ is outside of the domain of $t,$ so we discard it. \n\nThe solution $t=1$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only time when the object is stationary." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083080 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty < t < \\infty$.\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083080 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty <     t <    \\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083080 1074 "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while\n\n$$\ny'(t) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\ny'(0) = -1\\neq 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that when $t = 0$, the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083080 1074 "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0 .$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1 .\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $y'(0) = -1\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t = 0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083081 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186083081 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186083081 1074 "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while\n\n$$\ny'(t) 