Now, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx &= r\\cos\\theta\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=3\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=3\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\ty &= r\\sin\\theta\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 3\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 3\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186092119 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186092119 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\ln (\\cos t)) =-\\tan t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\sqrt{(1)^{2} + (-\\tan t)^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\sqrt{1 + \\tan^{2} t}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\sqrt{\\sec^{2} t}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\sec tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186092120 1071 "![](images/a4/a43a1658a16151c0306702959c50e8d0288077983ad50a084b39703e206469c0.png)\n\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ shown above. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$ given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186092120 1071 "![](images/a4/a43a1658a16151c0306702959c50e8d0288077983ad50a084b39703e206469c0.png)\n\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ shown above. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$ given that \n![](images/e3/e34192873ce94d5cfe9e01b330208021ee47d73646ea9734feb211f51241119c.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186092120 1074 "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/41/414cb72ff004fe4c5898a0e2c7b60b4b3651473e776388fad21efd8fc9ae6165.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $A\\mathbf{v},$ we need to rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b24d8d460b7833a49f2bd7183f0f4b5d22d94a7d3b1d3bb9eb51beff9eeb7ef6.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186092120 1074 "Consider the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ni^{′} &= Ai = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = j \\\\\nj^{′} &= Aj = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} =-i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/41/414cb72ff004fe4c5898a0e2c7b60b4b3651473e776388fad21efd8fc9ae6165.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nradians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $A\\mathbf{v}$, we need to rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nradians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b24d8d460b7833a49f2bd7183f0f4b5d22d94a7d3b1d3bb9eb51beff9eeb7ef6.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186092122 1071 "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = e^{5x +4}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186092122 1071 "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = e^{5x +4}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186092122 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = e^u, \\qquad u=5x+4\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =5\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{df}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= e^{u} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 5e^{u} \\\\\n&= 5e^{5x + 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186092122 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = e^u, \\qquad u=5x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5{e}^{u} \\\\  & =5{e}^{5x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186092123 1071 "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{x^{n}}{3^n\\sqrt{n}}=\\dfrac{x}{3}+\\frac{x^2}{3^2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{x^3}{3^3\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{x^4}{3^4\\cdot 2}+\\cdots \\,.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186092123 1071 "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{x^{n}}{3^n\\sqrt{n}}=\\dfrac{x}{3}+\\frac{x^2}{3^2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{x^3}{3^3\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{x^4}{3^4\\cdot 2}+\\cdots \\,$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186092123 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define\n\n$$\na_n= \\dfrac{x^{n}}{3^n\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to\\infty$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid \\frac{x^{n + 1}}{3^{n + 1}\\sqrt{n + 1}} \\cdot \\frac{3^{n}\\sqrt{n}}{x^{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid \\frac{\\sqrt{n}x}{3\\sqrt{n + 1}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\mid x\\begin{vmatrix}}{3}lim_(n → ∞)(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{n + 1}}) \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid x \\mid}{3} \\cdot 1 \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid x\\end{vmatrix}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L < 1$. In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mid x \\mid}{3}< 1\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n$\\mid x \\mid < 3$.\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $3$, and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-3,3)$.\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1$. So, when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid x &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=3$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=3$, we have\n$∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{x^{n}}{3^{n}\\sqrt{n}}|= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{3^{n}}{3^{n}\\sqrt{n}}; = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}; = 1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}} + ⋯$,\nwhich is divergent by the $p$-series test. So the endpoint $x=3$ is *not* included in our interval.\n- When $x=-3$, we have\n$∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{x^{n}}{3^{n}\\sqrt{n}}|= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-3)^{n})/(3^{n}\\sqrt{n}); = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n})/(\\sqrt{n}); =-1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}} + ⋯$,\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So the endpoint $x=-3$ *is* included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-3,3)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186092123 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define $a_n=  \\dfrac{x^{n}}{3^n\\sqrt{n}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to\\infty\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{3}^{n+1}\\sqrt{n+1}}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{n}\\sqrt{n}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\sqrt{n}x}{3\\sqrt{n+1}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{3}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{n+1}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L <    1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{|x|}{3}<   1\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n|x| < 3.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $3,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-3,3).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=3,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=3$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{3}^{n}\\sqrt{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n}}{{3}^{n}\\sqrt{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is divergent by the $p$-series test.  So the endpoint $x=3$ is *not* included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{3}^{n}\\sqrt{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-3)^{n}}{{3}^{n}\\sqrt{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=-3$ *is* included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-3,3).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186092124 1071 "Given that $y= 3\\cdot 2^{1 - x}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186092124 1071 "Given that $y= 3\\cdot 2^{1 - x},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" 13194139533389 1759218604558