e] [17592186096547 1071 "Differentiate $y = \\cos^4{2x}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186096547 1074 "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny = u^4, \\qquad u = \\cos{v}, \\qquad v = 2x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 4u^3,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d v} = -\\sin{v},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm d x} = 2\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dv} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dx} \\\\\n&= 4u^{3} \\cdot (-\\sin v) \\cdot (2) \\\\\n&= 4(\\cos 2x)^{3} \\cdot (-\\sin 2x) \\cdot (2) \\\\\n&=-8\\cos^{3} 2x\\sin 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186096547 1074 "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny = u^4, \\qquad u = \\cos{v}, \\qquad v = 2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 4u^3,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d v} = -\\sin{v},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm d x} = 2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =-8{cos}^{3}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186096548 1071 "Find $\\mathbf F(t)$ given that $\\mathbf F'(t)= \\langle 6t^5+2t, \\sec^2 t\\rangle$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186096548 1071 "Find $\\mathbf F(t)$ given that $\\mathbf F'(t)= \\langle 6t^5+2t, \\sec^2 t\\rangle.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186096548 1074 "Note that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle 6t^5+2t,\\, \\sec^2 t\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int⟨6t^{5} + 2t, \\sec^{2} t⟩dt &= ⟨\\int(6t^{5} + 2t)dt, \\int\\sec^{2} tdt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t^{6} + t^{2} + c_{1}, \\tan t + c_{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t^{6} + t^{2}, \\tan t⟩ + ⟨c_{1}, c_{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t^{6} + t^{2}, \\tan t⟩ + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186096548 1074 "Note that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle 6t^5+2t,\\, \\sec^2 t\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨6{t}^{5}+2t,\\,{sec}^{2}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int (6{t}^{5}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int {sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2}+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186096549 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\left\\langle\\cos(2t+1), \\, 4\\right\\rangle \\,\\textrm d t\\,$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186096549 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\left\\langle\\cos(2t+1), \\, 4\\right\\rangle \\,\\textrm d t\\,.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186096549 1074 "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1),\\,4⟩\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x(t) =\\cos(2t+1),$ we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u= 2t +1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d t = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)+{c}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1)+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t) =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int 4\\text{d}t=4t+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1),\\,4⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1)+{c}_{1},\\,4t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1),\\,4t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1),\\,4t⟩+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186096549 1074 "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\int⟨\\cos (2t + 1), 4⟩dt = ⟨\\int\\cos (2t + 1)dt, \\int4dt⟩\n$$\n\n- For $x(t) =\\cos(2t+1)$, we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u= 2t +1$. Then\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d t = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u$.\nTherefore, we have\n$\\intx(t)dt|= \\displaystyle \\int\\cos (2t + 1)dt; = \\displaystyle \\int\\cos u \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du; = \\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int\\cos udu; = \\frac{1}{2}(\\sin u) + c_{1}; = \\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2t + 1) + c_{1}$.\n- For $y(t) =4$, we have\n$\\inty(t)dt = \\displaystyle \\int4dt = 4t + c_{2}$.\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int⟨\\cos (2t + 1), 4⟩dt &= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2t + 1) + c_{1}, 4t + c_{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2t + 1), 4t⟩ + ⟨c_{1}, c_{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2t + 1), 4t⟩ + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186096550 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0