},-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos (2t) + c_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(0)=\\left\\langle 0,\\, 0 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(0) = ⟨\\sec 0 + c_{1},-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos (2 \\cdot 0) + c_{2}⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩ \\\\\n⟨1 + c_{1},-\\frac{1}{2} + c_{2}⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $c_1=-1$ and\n\n$$\nc_2=\\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\left\\langle \\sec t - 1, \\, -\\dfrac 1 2\\cos(2t)+ \\dfrac12\\right\\rangle\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186096551 1074 "Note that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle \\sec t \\tan t,\\,  \\sin(2t)\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+{c}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $\\mathbf F(0)=\\left\\langle 0,\\, 0 \\right\\rangle$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0)=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+{c}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1+{c}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨0,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=\\dfrac 12.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\left\\langle  \\sec t - 1  , \\,  -\\dfrac 1 2\\cos(2t)+ \\dfrac12\\right\\rangle.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186096552 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=2 \\\\ x-4z=6 \\\\ -3x+6y=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186096552 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n${x - 2y = 2; x - 4z = 6; - 3x + 6y =-6$\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186096552 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](images/89/89b1f5f2dbc7e2677b0f73445601a1c7280f218bd50b7e8607c9b2cb9d2d5179.png)\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/ed/ed183109cb48a15a2f1f2309b8d5e16525d781e37db1ebc8c3c528720e90e808.png)\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4z+6 \\\\ y=2z+2 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4z+6 \\\\ 2z+2 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186096552 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\nM\\begin{vmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{2}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\nM\\begin{vmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{1} + 2R_{2}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\nx - 4z = 6; y - 2z = 2; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system.\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z$. That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z$.\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{x = 4z + 6; y = 2z + 2; z = z\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the 