)$ by substituting the solution into $z=e^y.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & =z \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & =x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$ So, the final solution is\n\n$$\ny = x - \\ln (x), \\quad x > 0.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100765 1074 "Let's make a substitution\n$z=e^y\\,$.\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}=e^y\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}+z=e^x\\,\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition\n\n$$\nz(1)=e^{y(1)}=e^1=e\\,\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side of the differential equation is now an exact derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx\\frac{dz}{dx} + z &= \\frac{d}{dx}(xz)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xz) &= e^{x} \\\\\n\\int\\frac{d}{dx}(xz)dx &= \\inte^{x}dx \\\\\nxz &= e^{x} + C \\\\\nz &= \\frac{e^{x} + C}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the value of $C$ that satisfies the initial condition $z(1)=e$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz(1) &= e \\\\\n\\frac{e^{1} + C}{1} &= e \\\\\ne + C &= e \\\\\nC &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\nz = \\frac{e^x}{x}\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=e^y$. This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{y} &= z \\\\\ne^{y} &= \\frac{e^{x}}{x} \\\\\ny &= \\ln (\\frac{e^{x}}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\ln (e^{x}) - \\ln (x) \\\\\n&= x - \\ln (x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0$. So, the final solution is\n\n$$\ny = x - \\ln (x), \\quad x > 0\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100766 1071 "Find the general solution to the following first-order ODE using the substitution $z=y^3$.\n$xy^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = x^2+y^3$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100766 1071 "Find the general solution to the following first-order ODE using the substitution $z=y^3.$\n\n$$\nxy^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = x^2+y^3\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100766 1074 "Let's make a substitution\n\n$$\nz=y^3\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}=3y^2\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\n Consequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)=x^2+z\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3}{x}({x}^{2}+z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3x+\\frac{3}{x}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{3}{x}\\,z & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-\\frac{3}{x},\\,Q(x)=3x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{3}{x}\\,z) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 3x \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,z & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{z}{{x}^{3}}) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{z}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z}{{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{3}{x}+C \\\\ z & =-3{x}^{2}+C{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=y^3.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3} & =z \\\\ {y}^{3} & =-3{x}^{2}+C{x}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{-3{x}^{2}+C{x}^{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100766 1074 "Let's make a substitution\n$z=y^3\\,$.\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}=3y^2\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)=x^2+z\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} &= \\frac{3}{x}(x^{2} + z) \\\\\n\\frac{dz}{dx} &= 3x + \\frac{3}{x}z \\\\\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - \\frac{3}{x}z &= 3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) =-\\frac{3}{x}, Q(x) = 3x\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{\\intP(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\int(-3/x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{-3\\ln x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^3}\n$$\n\nyields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{x^{3}}(\\frac{dz}{dx} - \\frac{3}{x}z) &= \\frac{1}{x^{3}} \\cdot 3x \\\\\n\\frac{1}{x^{3}} \\cdot \\frac{dz}{dx} - \\frac{3}{x^{4}}z &= \\frac{3}{x^{2}} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{z}{x^{3}}) &= \\frac{3}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{z}{x^{3}})dx &= \\int\\frac{3}{x^{2}}dx \\\\\n\\frac{z}{x^{3}} &= -\\frac{3}{x} + C \\\\\nz &= -3x^{2} + Cx^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=y^3$. This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{3} &= z \\\\\ny^{3} &= -3x^{2} + Cx^{3} \\\\\ny &= \\sqrt[3]{-3x^{2} + Cx^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100767 1071 "For the piecewise function\n$f(x) = {4x^{3} - 2\\begin{vmatrix}forx < 1 \\\\ 3x + b\\end{vmatrix}forx > 1$,\nit is known that $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}f(x)$ exists. What is the value of the constant $b$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100767 1071 "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ 3x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned},\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}f(x)$ exists. What is the value of the constant $b?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100767 1074 "In order for $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}f(x)$ to exist, the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 1$, so we use the expression $f(x)=4x^3-2$. We get\n$lim_(x → 1^{-})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{-})4x^{3} - 2; = 4(1)^{3} - 2; = 2$.\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1$, so we use the expression $f(x)= 3x+b$. We get\n$lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{+})3x + b; = 3(1) + b; = 3 + b$.\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x) \\\\\n2 &= 3 + b \\\\\nb &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100767 1074 "In order for $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}f(x)$ to exist, the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=4x^3-2.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}4{x}^{3}-2 \\\\  & =4(1)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)= 3x+b.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}3x+b \\\\  & =3(1)+b \\\\  & =3+b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 2 & =3+b \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100771 1071 "The substitution $z= y^2$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} = \\dfrac{x+2}{2y} + 2x^2y\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100771 1071 "The substitution $z= y^2$ can be used to transform the equation\n$\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} = \\dfrac{x+2}{2y} + 2x^2y$\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $P(x)$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100771 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2z^{1/2}}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2{z}^{1/2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+2}{2{z}^{1/2}}+2{x}^{2}{z}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =x+2+4{x}^{2}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-4{x}^{2}z & =x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x) = - 4x^2.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100771 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n$z= y^{2}$.\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2z^{1/2}}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2z^{1/2}}\\frac{dz}{dx} &= \\frac{x + 2}{2z^{1/2}} + 2x^{2}z^{1/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} &= x + 2 + 4x^{2}z \\\\\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - 4x^{2}z &= x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(x) =-4x^2\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100774 1071 "What is the coefficient of $(x+2)^2$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ at $x = -2$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100774 1071 "What is the coefficient of $(x+2)^2$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ at $x = -2?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100774 1074 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nP_{2}(x) = f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x + 2) + (f^{″}(-2))/(2)(x + 2)^{2}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x+2)^2$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{f''(-2)}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative $f''(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(x)&=\\dfrac{1}{x} \\\\ f'(x)&=-\\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\\\ f''(x)&=\\dfrac{2}{x^3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate $f''(x)$ at $x=-2$. This gives\n\n$$\nf''(-2)=\\dfrac{2}{(-2)^3}=-\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x+2)^2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{f''(-2)}{2}&=\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)}{2}=-\\dfrac{1}{8}. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100774 1074 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x)=f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x+2)^2$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{f''(-2)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(x)&=\\dfrac{1}{x}\\\n\t\tf'(x)&=-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\\n\t\tf''(x)&=\\dfrac{2}{x^3}\n\t\t\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate $f''(x)$ at $x=-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nf''(-2)=\\dfrac{2}{(-2)^3}=-\\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x+2)^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\dfrac{f''(-2)}{2}&=\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)}{2}=-\\dfrac{1}{8}.\n}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100775 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be a function with $f(-1)=-1, f'(-1)=3, f''(-1)=-6$ and $f'''(-1)=6$. What is the third-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100775 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be a function with $f(-1)=-1, f'(-1)=3, f''(-1)=-6$ and $f'''(-1)=6.$ What is the third-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100775 1074 "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{3}(x) &= f(-1) + f^{′}(-1)(x + 1) + (f^{″}(-1))/(2!)(x + 1)^{2} + (f^{‴}(-1))/(3!)(x + 1)^{3} \\\\\n&= f(-1) + f^{′}(-1)(x + 1) + (f^{″}(-1))/(2)(x + 1)^{2} + (f^{‴}(-1))/(6)(x + 1)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have been given the following set of values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= -1 \\\\\nf^{′}(-1) &= 3 \\\\\nf^{″}(-1) &= -6 \\\\\nf^{‴}(-1) &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our Taylor polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{3}(x) &= -1 + 3(x + 1) + ((-6))/(2)(x + 1)^{2} + \\frac{6}{6}(x + 1)^{3} \\\\\n&=-1 + 3(x + 1) - 3(x + 1)^{2} + (x + 1)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100775 1074 "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(-1)+{f}^{′}(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-1)}{2!}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(-1)}{3!}(x+1)^{3} \\\\  & =f(-1)+{f}^{′}(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-1)}{2}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(-1)}{6}(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have been given the following set of values: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-1) & =-6 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-1) & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our Taylor polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =-1+3(x+1)+\\frac{(-6)}{2}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{6}{6}(x+1)^{3} \\\\  & =-1+3(x+1)-3(x+1)^{2}+(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100783 1071 "The growth rate of the hamster population $P(t)$ in a park is jointly proportional to the hamster population at time $t$, measured in years, and the difference between the park's carrying capacity and the hamster population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum possible hamster population is $120$, which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100783 1071 "The growth rate of the hamster population $P(t)$ in a park is jointly proportional to the hamster population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the park's carrying capacity and the hamster population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum possible hamster population is $120,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100783 1074 "Since the hamster population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hamster population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hamster population, the hamster population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005$, and the carrying capacity is $a=120$. Therefore, the hamster population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.005P(120-P)\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100783 1074 "Since the hamster population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hamster population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hamster population, the hamster population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=120.$ Therefore, the hamster population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.005P(120-P).\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100784 1071 "Find the second degree Taylor polynomial of the function $f(x) = e^{2x}$ about $x=1$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100784 1071 "Find the second degree Taylor polynomial of the function $f(x) = e^{2x}$ about $x=1.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100784 1074 "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=1$, we plug $a=1$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\nP_2(x) = f(1) + f'(1)(x-1) +\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(1)(x-1)^2\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f(1)$, $f'(1)$, and $f''(1)\\mathbin{;}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(x) &= e^{2x} & ⟹ & f(1)|= e^{2} \\\\ f^{′}(x)|= 2e^{2x} & ⟹ & f^{′}(1)|= 2e^{2} \\\\ f^{″}(x)|= 4e^{2x} & ⟹ & f^{″}(1)|= 4e^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(1)$, $f'(1)$, and $f''(1)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{2}(x) &= e^{2} + 2e^{2}(x - 1) + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4e^{2}(x - 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= e^{2} + 2e^{2}(x - 1) + 2e^{2}(x - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe plot below shows $f(x)$, $P_2(x)$, and $L(x)$. We can see that $P_2(x)$ is a better approximation to $f(x)$ around $x=1$ compared with $L(x)$.\n\n![](images/a7/a72eb0b9a50cdc0f8867b6f86e9273fb86502ad6060abffc34f32ae536722ac7.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100784 1074 "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=1,$ we plug $a=1$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\nP_2(x) = f(1) + f'(1)(x-1) +\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(1)(x-1)^2.\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f(1),$ $f'(1),$ and $f''(1)\\mathbin{;}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & f(1) & ={e}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(1) & =2{e}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(1) & =4{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(1),$ $f'(1),$ and $f''(1)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & ={e}^{2}+2{e}^{2}(x-1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4{e}^{2}(x-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2{e}^{2}(x-1)+2{e}^{2}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe plot below shows $f(x),$ $P_2(x),$ and $L(x).$ We can see that $P_2(x)$ is a better approximation to $f(x)$ around $x=1$ compared with $L(x).$\n\n![](images/a7/a72eb0b9a50cdc0f8867b6f86e9273fb86502ad6060abffc34f32ae536722ac7.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100785 1071 "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56-7y^2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y^2-7$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186100785 1071 "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56-7y^2$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y^2-7$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100785 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 7y(8-y)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality $k=7$, and the carrying capacity is $a=8$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186100785 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 7y(8-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality $k=7,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=8.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105634 1071 "![](images/fd/fdfa0528b5476ed0246679c45fd31824470063a68f33c4c9af0f714ad201538f.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2$. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105634 1071 "![](images/fd/fdfa0528b5476ed0246679c45fd31824470063a68f33c4c9af0f714ad201538f.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2.$ Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105634 1074 "The eigenvector ![](images/9f/9f9c7bc79c4e294e45f34d9e10c0e429c33ce95730d13aaaf337f7a9cb22f1a3.png) corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av=2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/78/78e1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105634 1074 "The eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2$. So,\n$Av = 2v$.\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/78/78e1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105651 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 19 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105651 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n![](images/a3/a39dfc581111c2601dd5851c16fccc086a980a84b7804ef0d0800252016dce83.png)\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105651 1074 "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\nM\\begin{vmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 19 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} - R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} - R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=\\frac{1}{3}R_{2}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} + R_{2}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{x - 2y - 6z = 7; 0x + y + 2z = 4; 0x + 0y + 0z = 3\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation\n\n$$\n0x+0y+0z = 3\n$$\n\nwe can conclude that our system has no solution." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105651 1074 "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 19 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -8 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -8 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y-6z=7 \\\\ 0x+y+2z=4 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z = 3,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105657 1071 "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped $6$ times\n\n- The number of times a $6$-sided die is rolled until a $6$ appears\n\n- The number of months in a particular year" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105657 1071 "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n1. The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped $6$ times\n2. The number of times a $6$-sided die is rolled until a $6$ appears\n3. The number of months in a particular year" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105657 1074 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can count its possible values in a sequence: $1,2,3,\\ldots$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a discrete random variable. The number of months in a particular year is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $12$ months in a year.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105657 1074 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6$.\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can count its possible values in a sequence: $1,2,3,\\ldots$\n- Statement III does *not* describe a discrete random variable. The number of months in a particular year is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $12$ months in a year.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105658 1071 "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n![](images/9e/9e8f3f1bb50432add6ba6c1b91fb5e1f7ab052e48ab155c2e92b40bc309e81a5.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105658 1071 "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{2}{5},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5},\\end{vmatrix}x = 1; \\frac{4}{5}, \\mid x = 2g(x) = {\\frac{2}{5},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{10},\\end{vmatrix}x = 1h(x) = {\\frac{3}{10},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2},\\end{vmatrix}x = 1; \\frac{1}{5}, \\mid x = 2$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105658 1074 "For a function ![](images/93/9363d37d53374519f1170d2c1c24ecb690a6810a304207ef21a0d1f3eba609de.png) to be a valid probability mass function on a set ![](images/5c/5c3d9b70452a08b50ba5b826f5694c3d85c999754e5304ff71d47362a66534cd.png) it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- ![](images/53/53ad6d309b94b7889b85ecc5d90ae0201d2ba7b25b2b82cc9b6c95d1165ba268.png) for all ![](images/86/86d18b1a03e111c64a7328566ad27a5ae12870cf2ee67e9750f5747bee71eb86.png) in ![](images/d4/d4aaadce376b13dc707757d84b05abf9c95d436aa2e4bea8fb19a078eff136c6.png)\n\n- ![](images/02/024a7717c9fccd8dceb278a0a50934b514706a425b43f76ee9d229b9008b822b.png)\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function ![](images/06/06a568b15033fbfbfc33c59b4f4c1b9382df53efb53025fa9bdf5714972c7440.png) is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value ![](images/d1/d14540db55ffd098075a55e4c744e9cde80545517df1456959c8baea7500586e.png) does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between ![](images/63/637d7cec1da56f96138c07b108310c3d842ddc13b466234f78b4ee1757882a1b.png) and ![](images/5d/5d9e7c41ca12f816fdb84db36a35ba66280982891484cbee9db4a7222c863d92.png)).\n\n- The function ![](images/3d/3d7612998c8458ff973fe19d9fa000175d6ab8ce6d2689d593852f0311e73b6b.png) is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of ![](images/49/49f6627729021cf5e3e78f4dcbedb957495a385bccdb4484e5a5ac6f892b0161.png) do not sum to ![](images/19/19ef2d374cfb64bc575f920e7413112e512fdda510e0fa71dfbd8297cf05d96d.png)\n![](images/cb/cb3ebaf420b7fc49cf896463ad37b95855da99cc6356e4795dc81df2d76f5503.png)\n\n- The function ![](images/70/70bff1a95629c275ea93a10e6130b14e6565bb70465a778e1bff38526ad389da.png) is a valid probability distribution. Indeed, we have\n![](images/53/5392675a2894798e20c813a2bc0611940d9f37373f85ed4e474016d673d9a5e3.png)\nand\n![](images/10/10c830df4267667cf3475f1a41b45d5ec27949c1395b00b7502325481dc93830.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"![](images/3d/3d8cbf1521bc8f20e8ad2dff7bf427089ee3e1e591cdba51102049daab5d70f5.png) only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105658 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value $f(1)=-\\dfrac15$ does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between $0$ and $1$).\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $h$ do not sum to $1{:}$\n$\\dfrac25 + \\dfrac{3}{10} = \\dfrac{7}{10} \\neq 1$\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability distribution. Indeed, we have\n$0 \\leq \\dfrac{3}{10}, \\dfrac12, \\dfrac15 \\leq 1$\nand\n$\\dfrac{3}{10} + \\dfrac12 + \\dfrac15 = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105659 1071 "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ |\n\nCompute $P\\left( X \\leq 3 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105659 1071 "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ |\n\nCompute $P\\left(X \\leq 3 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105659 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= P(x ∈ {1, 2, 3}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) + P(X = 3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{3}{10} + \\frac{1}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105659 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(x\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105660 1071 "| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P\\left(X\\in \\{2,4,6\\}\\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105660 1071 "| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P\\left(X\\in \\{2,4,6\\}\\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105660 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X ∈ {2, 4, 6}) &= P(X = 2) + P(X = 4) + P(X = 6) \\\\\n&= f(2) + f(4) + f(6) \\\\\n&= 0.1 + 0.1 + 0.2 \\\\\n&= 0.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105660 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\in {2,4,6}) & =P(X=2)+P(X=4)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(4)+f(6) \\\\  & =0.1+0.1+0.2 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105665 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of students that passed the exam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=5$ students\n\n- the probability that each student passed the exam is $p=75\\%=0.75$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(5,0.75).$ We want to find the probability that $x=3$ of the students passed the exam.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})(0.75)^{3}(1-0.75)^{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!}(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =10(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =0.264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105665 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of students that passed the exam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=5$ students\n- the probability that each student passed the exam is $p=75\\%=0.75$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(5,0.75)$. We want to find the probability that $x=3$ of the students passed the exam.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 3) &= (\\frac{5}{3})(0.75)^{3}(1 - 0.75)^{5 - 3} \\\\\n&= (5!)/(3!(5 - 3)!)(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\\n&= 10(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.264\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105666 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ rolls\n\n- the probability of rolling an even number on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{3}{6} = 0.5\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(8,0.5).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(1-0.5)^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of rolling an even number at least $7$ times. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 7) & =P(X=7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8})(0.5)^{8}(0.5)^{8-8} \\\\  & =8(0.5)^{7}(0.5)+1(0.5)^{8} \\\\  & =0.035\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105666 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ rolls\n- the probability of rolling an even number on any roll of the die is\n$p=\\dfrac{3}{6} = 0.5$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(8,0.5)$.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = x) &= (\\frac{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(1 - 0.5)^{8 - x} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{8 - x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of rolling an even number at least $7$ times. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 7) &= P(X = 7) + P(X = 8) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{8}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{8 - 7} + (\\frac{8}{8})(0.5)^{8}(0.5)^{8 - 8} \\\\\n&= 8(0.5)^{7}(0.5) + 1(0.5)^{8} \\\\\n&= 0.035\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105667 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The ages of $20$ college professors\n\n- The number of questions answered correctly by a student in a $12$-question exam\n\n- The heights of $10$ elementary school children" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105667 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n1. The ages of $20$ college professors\n2. The number of questions answered correctly by a student in a $12$-question exam\n3. The heights of $10$ elementary school children" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186105667 1074 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of questions answered in a $12$- question exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $12$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The heights cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186105667 1074 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of questions answered in a $12$- question exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $12$ independent experiments.\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The heights cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186115130 1071 "Given the system\n![](images/42/427c19efbebba6bfe2dc36bccd852d7a1f5e0ec97dc46c00043d019807e86ec2.png)\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n\n- The system has no solution\n\n- The system is consistent independent" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186115130 1071 "Given the system\n$2x - 3y = 4; - 4x + 6y =-8$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The system is consistent dependent\n2. The system has no solution\n3. The system is consistent independent" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186115130 1074 "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](images/50/50f13deaa03c2af30f511ef3d514e3dd2e118d81d9b121b436ff1a2f2497c303.png)\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](images/e0/e02bb5004f792eea20216e64098bb924dbed15defb9330967d6ae5a144e067dc.png)\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x,$ and we have a free variable $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186115130 1074 "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + 2R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n2x - 3y = 4; 0x + 0y = 0\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation\n\n$$\n0x+0y=0\n$$\n\nwe can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x$, and we have a free variable $y$. That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$.\n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186115131 1071 "Given the matrix\n![](images/a3/a334702f22fbd5dd62d8584d4f3e16482c1ce85da7167902294e45873cfe338d.png) find the row operations that transform $A$ into the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186115131 1071 "Given the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ find the row operations that transform $A$ into the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186115131 1074 "We consider the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,I]$ where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity. Applying the indicated row operations we have\n![](images/cd/cd018c9d74dfe459131b33723d784af1cd97434f812a8bc8142da5832b943b95.png)\n\nTherefore, the required row operations are $R_3 := (-1) R_3$ and $R_1 := R_1+(-4)R_3,$ and inverse matrix is \n![](images/c5/c5b78f969ba52a5a19ac41ddbde2f5623c70af98d4cca190b2df93b4a9bd7071.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186115131 1074 "We consider the augmented matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}A\\, & \\,I\\end{bmatrix}$ where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity. Applying the indicated row operations we have\n\n$$\nExtra \\left or missing \\right\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operations are\n\n$$\nR_3:= (-1) R_3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nR_1:= R_1+(-4)R_3\n$$\n\nand inverse matrix is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119670 1071 "Find a normal vector to the plane\n$\\mathbf{r}= \\langle -6,3, 2 \\rangle + t \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle + s \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle,\\qquad t,\\,s \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119670 1071 "Find a normal vector to the plane \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}= \\langle -6,3, 2 \\rangle + t \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle + s \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle,\\qquad t,\\,s \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186119670 1074 "From the parametric equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{w} = \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nare parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= v \\cdot w \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\\\\n&= 4 \\mid - 3 \\mid 2 \\\\\n&= i - 12j - 20k \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-12,-20⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 1, -12, -20 \\rangle\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119670 1074 "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{w} = \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i-12j-20k \\\\  & =⟨1,-12,-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 1, -12, -20 \\rangle.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186119671 1071 "Given that $\\det(X)=\\det(Y)$, which elementary row operations transforms matrix $X$ to matrix $Y$, where\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119671 1071 "Given that $\\det(X)=\\det(Y),$ which elementary row operations transforms matrix $X$ to matrix $Y,$ where\n\n![](images/36/3692f985d592f9680ca9fbf38991df32c7665ae02c7dae04fec6062e3b62884d.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186119671 1074 "Since\n\n$$\n\\det(X)=\\det(Y)\n$$\n\n, the row operation applied to $X$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form\n\n$$\nR_j:= R_j + (k)R_i, \\quad i \\neq j\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(X) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\mid R_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + 3R_{3} \\\\ =\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2 \\\\\n&= - 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= det(Y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + 3R_3\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119671 1074 "Since $\\det(X)=\\det(Y)$, the row operation applied to $X$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j + (k)R_i, \\quad i \\neq j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(X) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + 3R_3.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186119672 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times4$ matrix and $\\det(A)=5$. What is the value of $\\det(2A)$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119672 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times4$ matrix and $\\det(A)=5.$ What is the value of $\\det(2A)?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186119672 1074 "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k$, and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\ndet(k \\cdot A) = k^{n} \\cdot det(A)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=4$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(2A) &= 2^{4} \\cdot det(A) \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 80\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119672 1074 "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(2A) & ={2}^{4}\\cdot det(A) \\\\  & =16\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186119673 1071 "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}9x+3y-6z=8 \\\\ -6x+4y+2z=3 \\\\ 5x-5y-15z=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=\\dfrac{D_x}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and $D_x=$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186119673 1071 "Consider the system of equations\n${9x + 3y - 6z = 8; - 6x + 4y + 2z = 3; 5x - 5y - 15z = 10$.\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=\\dfrac{D_x}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and $D_x=$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119673 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{9x + 3y + (-6)z = 8; (-6)x + 4y + 2z = 3; 5x + (-5)y + (-15)z = 10\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & -15\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -5 & -15\\end{vmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119673 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}9x+3y+(-6)z=8 \\\\ (-6)x+4y+2z=3 \\\\ 5x+(-5)y+(-15)z=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n![](images/00/00a69fe1d48b41e376971f3669ae51668733d36dfda18c0f9cc736d5d071a441.png)\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n![](images/14/14e850c9af7c0b2fdd1e36299667b2cbe91c84e8bb90030b729ee23b296ad984.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186119674 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A$?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119674 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- ![](images/f0/f07498a45503a02fa9fb4d3ecfd9bdae54a363b632152016f2f4e5c3f6170656.png)\n\n- ![](images/30/300278e3edb64ab9dcb945668b923f385b9873be5bd33264a7ed10f6072262b7.png)\n\n- ![](images/e2/e26513d3753cd1437a42a9f57f160fb25e2fb9b84e1df1db07579429749eea2c.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186119674 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A$, we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although the left matrix is a lower triangular matrix, it is not a *unit* lower triangular matrix because it does not have $1$'s on the diagonal.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119674 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n![](images/98/98d64291b8e0d4effe6bb1f3006c3d88d2687697355a83c1b092cdfb54ba4188.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n![](images/10/10a251d4445a3b190e92fa179badfb5e989b577f3c88812334e921522e7a20e5.png)\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although the left matrix is a lower triangular matrix, it is not a *unit* lower triangular matrix because it does not have $1$'s on the diagonal.\n![](images/51/515f139b62a15ee4b38066e6225066b527e277e904ea97473d2a9f50f0f595cb.png)\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n![](images/e3/e377b0ff63ac6d7b3938187e47a3b5bca0087ddb6eee1d2c086835f394cdf6b9.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186119675 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n![](images/9f/9f5f9de266fac1e593ea58a3406ff88ccecb3a64963d55367297404360771d83.png)\n\nIf $x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf b,$ then what is $x_1+x_2+x_3?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186119675 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf $x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf b$, then what is $x_1+x_2+x_3$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119675 1074 "Since $\\mathbf{b}$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a_1}, \\,\\mathbf{a_2}$ and $\\mathbf{a_3}$ with coefficients $x_1,\\, x_2$ and $x_3$, respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} &= b \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} + x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ x_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}x_{3} \\\\ 2x_{3} \\\\ 3x_{3}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} + x_{3} \\\\ x_{1} + 2x_{3} \\\\ -x_{2} + 3x_{3}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{2} + x_{3} = 1; x_{1} + 2x_{3} = 1; - x_{2} + 3x_{3} = 7\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} & ↔R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=\\frac{1}{4}R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 2x_{3} = 1; x_{2} + x_{3} = 1; x_{3} = 2\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 = 2$. Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_2 = -1\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_3=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+2(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_1 = -3\n$$\n\nSo the solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx_1=-3, x_2=-1, x_3=2\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n-3 \\mathbf{a_1}- \\mathbf{a_2} +2 \\mathbf{a_3}= \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2+x_3=-3-1+2=-2\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119675 1074 "Since $\\mathbf{b}$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a_1}, \\,\\mathbf{a_2}$ and $\\mathbf{a_3}$ with coefficients $x_1,\\, x_2$ and $x_3,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n![](images/4d/4d3cb5eb82dd012582c78fc2c1201c62f99779d92974aba2a61f5a6bce8b91b9.png)\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ {x}_{3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 = 2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_2 = -1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_3=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+2(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_1 = -3.\n$$\n\nSo the solution of the system is $x_1=-3, x_2=-1, x_3=2,$ which means\n\n$$\n-3 \\mathbf{a_1}- \\mathbf{a_2} +2 \\mathbf{a_3}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2+x_3=-3-1+2=-2.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186119676 1071 "Consider the matrix given by ![](images/75/7565496e57b9dede7d994f9e8ee936e158f67cc8c7397e182c9c6e940939f823.png) Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector ![](images/b3/b3be9df23c26f562cd1e7c5e1162e87bcc7da0085c00b0185221a6ae45f11395.png) belongs to $\\textrm{Col}(A).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186119676 1071 "Consider the matrix given by $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 + k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Col}(A)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119676 1074 "If $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, then the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nmust be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}A \\, & \\, \\mathbf{b}\\end{bmatrix}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}A & b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 + k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} - 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -32 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 + k\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} - 5R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -32 \\\\ 0 & -11 & k - 48\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=\\frac{1}{8}R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -11 & k - 48\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\