\sin t)^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int_{0}^{2π}‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\sqrt{2}dt \\\\\n&= \\left.\\sqrt{2}t\\right|_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}(2π - 0) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186119681 1074 "First, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\sqrt{2}\\,t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186124366 1071 "![](images/99/99b941869c761efa0441ec83c5aec729d31b3f8294ec09e1ad5daa828f707d50.png)\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186124366 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186124366 1074 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 - λ & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}4 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)((4 - λ)(4 - λ) - 0) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)(λ - 4)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=1$ (simple root) and $\\lambda_2=4$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root $\\lambda_1 = 1$. Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace $V_{1}$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = 1$ is\n$\\text{dim}(V_{1}) = 1$.\n- Next, we consider the double root $\\lambda_2 = 4$. Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace $V_{4}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-4I$:\n$A - 4I|= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} - 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1}; R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda_2 = 4$. So,\n$\\text{dim}(V_{4}) = 2$.\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, since\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{1}) + \\text{dim}(V_{4}) = 1+2 = 3. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nFinally, a corresponding diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186124366 1074 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-0) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=1$ (simple root) and $\\lambda_2=4$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root $\\lambda_1 = 1.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace $V_{1}$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{1}) = 1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root $\\lambda_2 = 4.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace $V_{4}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-4I \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda_2 = 4.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{4}) = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, since\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{1}) + \\text{dim}(V_{4}) = 1+2 = 3. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nFinally, a corresponding diagonal matrix is \n\n![](images/51/513efa38c61e97f8b8b2a777488deb28ec551de06567f8c97fa6e59527bcd8e7.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186124367 1071 "Given that ![](images/cb/cb48b48ef82e5d6cfe903488e80a8cbd48e286c864ba387450e01430e718d77c.png) find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186124367 1071 "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186124367 1074 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(λ) &= λ^{2} - tr(A)λ + det(A)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda)$, i.e., the eigenvalues of $A$, satisfy\n\n$$\n{λ_{1} + λ_{2} = tr(A); λ_{1} \\cdot λ_{2} = det(A)\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2 = \\textrm{tr}(A)= 9+(-6)=3\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186124367 1074 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda),$ i.e., the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2 = \\textrm{tr}(A)= 9+(-6)=3.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186124368 1071 "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ for the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{12y}{x^2-y^2}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186124368 1071 "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ for the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{12y}{x^2-y^2}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186124368 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $G$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)&= \\dfrac{12(1)}{\\big(\\sqrt{5}\\big)^2 - 1^2} = 3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = c\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3$. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= c \\\\\n\\frac{12y}{x^{2} - y^{2}} &= 3 \\\\\n4y &= x^{2} - y^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - y^{2} - 4y - 4 &= -4 \\\\\nx^{2} - (y^{2} + 4y + 4) &= -4 \\\\\nx^{2} - (y + 2)^{2} &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the hyperbola\n\n$$\nx^2 - (y+2)^2 = -4\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186124368 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $G$:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n  f\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)&= \\dfrac{12(1)}{\\big(\\sqrt{5}\\big)^2 - 1^2} = 3}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ 4y & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4y-4 & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-({y}^{2}+4y+4) & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-(y+2)^{2} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the hyperbola $x^2 - (y+2)^2 = -4.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186124369 1071 "![](images/16/165917feacd28344502759fc6e03f96c5b54d9530701916b746fc489374a6d0b.png) \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $\\mathbf{x}_0$ given above. Calculate the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186124369 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $\\mathbf{x}_0$ given above. Calculate the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186124369 1074 "To find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2$, we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x_1}=P\\mathbf{x}_0, \\qquad \\mathbf{x}_2=P\\mathbf{x}_1\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= Px_{0} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= Px_{1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.172 \\\\ 0.828\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186124369 1074 "To find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2,$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x_1}=P\\mathbf{x}_0, \\qquad \\mathbf{x}_2=P\\mathbf{x}_1\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.172 \\\\ 0.828\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186124370 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets\n$-\\dfrac{(x-5)^2}{8} + \\dfrac{y^2}{50} + (z-3)^2 = -1$\nand the plane $z = 4$ onto the $xy$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186124370 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets \n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{(x-5)^2}{8} + \\dfrac{y^2}{50} + (z-3)^2 = -1\n$$\n\nand the plane $z = 4$ onto the $xy$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186124370 1074 "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $z = 4$, we substitute $z = 4$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- ((x - 5)^{2})/(