et\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda=1\\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186128982 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I$:\n\n﻿﻿﻿$\\begin{bmatrix}1 - λ & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)(1 - λ) - 2(-2) &= 0 \\\\\n1 - 2λ + λ^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 2λ + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-2) ± \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} - 4(1)(5)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± 4\\textrm{i}}{2} \\\\\n&= 1 ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda=1\\pm 2\\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186128983 1071 "Given that $\\lambda_1=-2+3\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_2$ are eigenvalues of a real $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$, find $2(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186128983 1071 "Given that $\\lambda_1=-2+3\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_2$ are eigenvalues of a real $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find $2(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186128983 1074 "Since\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-2+3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis a complex eigenvalue of $A$, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1$.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda}_1=-2-3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(λ_{1} + λ_{2}) &= 2(-2 + 3\\textrm{i} + (-2 - 3\\textrm{i})) \\\\\n&= 2(-4) \\\\\n&=-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 fa