(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-z & =0 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z = x^2+\\dfrac{y^2}{{1/4}}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186128988 1071 "Let ![](images/72/72d318ad7ca3ea0dff8b598a3c7126fd48a48f25fb6bdfec859e91701ffb9d15.png) and ![](images/75/75c63b77244e0233dd11874e55a1ab674f6149d49b8fdd3c939afa1cf016ca6e.png) be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186128988 1071 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=2$, respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186128988 1074 "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$, the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}e^{2t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186128988 1074 "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186128989 1071 "Evaluate the repeated integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{4}  \\int_{0}^{2} \\dfrac{x^3}{y^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x  \\, \\textrm{d}y.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186128989 1071 "Evaluate the repeated integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{4} \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2} \\dfrac{x^3}{y^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\, \\textrm{d}y$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186128989 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{2}^{4}[\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{x^{3}}{y^{2}}dx]dy &= \\int_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{y^{2}}[\\frac{x^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{y^{2}}(\\frac{2^{4}}{4} - \\frac{0^{4}}{4})dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{2}^{4}\\frac{4}{y^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 4\\int_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{y^{2}}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\int_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{y^{2}}dy &= 4[- \\frac{1}{y}]_{2}^{4} \\\\\n&= 4(-\\frac{1}{4} - (-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 fal