rt{3}\\cos (π)\\sin (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3} \\cdot (-1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&=-\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\\sin (π)\\sin (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3} \\cdot 0 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= ρ\\cos ϕ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3} \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-\\sqrt3, 0, -3)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129011 1071 "The point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt2,0,\\sqrt2)$. What are the spherical polar coordinates of $P$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129011 1071 "The point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt2,0,\\sqrt2).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of  $P?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129011 1074 "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho$, $\\theta$, and $\\phi$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}, \\qquad\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad\n\\cos\\phi = \\dfrac{z}{\\rho}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{0}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+0+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y) = (-\\sqrt2,0)$ lies on the negative $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, for $\\phi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(2, \\pi,  \\dfrac{\\pi}4 \\right).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129011 1074 "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho$, $\\theta$, and $\\phi$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}, \\qquad \\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad \\cos\\phi = \\dfrac{z}{\\rho}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2} + 0^{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2 + 0 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (-\\sqrt2,0)\n$$\n\nlies on the negative $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $\\phi$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ &= \\arccos (\\frac{z}{ρ}) \\\\\n&= \\arccos (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(2, \\pi, \\dfrac{\\pi}4 \\right)\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129012 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129012 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/ce/ce4692599c5c9fe69ed9247d65dee5d982e1b7a2cf33b9070b799c94c321bad4.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129012 1074 "Given a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nx=(u+v)^3\n$$\n\nand $y=(u-v)^2$.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= 3(u + v)^{2} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|= 3(u + v)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= 2(u - v) & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|=-2(u - v)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}3(u + v)^{2} & 3(u + v)^{2} \\\\ 2(u - v) & -2(u - v)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3(u + v)^{2} \\cdot (-2(u - v)) - 3(u + v)^{2} \\cdot 2(u - v) \\\\\n&=-12(u + v)^{2}(u - v)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129012 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/59/59265be9c026880542032006b0c4aec9f1c8eeb2cd448423d38c17f75e1393cd.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=(u+v)^3$ and $y=(u-v)^2.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u+v)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3(u+v)^{2} & 3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ 2(u-v) & -2(u-v)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2(u-v))-3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 2(u-v) \\\\  & =-12(u+v)^{2}(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129013 1071 "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{C}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = 8,\n\\qquad\n\\iint\\limits_{D}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = -5,\n$$\n\nfind the value of \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{C \\cup D}  3f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129013 1071 "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n$\\iint\\limits_{C} f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = 8, \\qquad \\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A = -5$,\nfind the value of\n$\\iint\\limits_{C \\cup D} 3f(x,y)\\ \\textrm{d}A$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129013 1074 "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(C∪D)3f(x, y)dA &= 3∬_(C∪D)f(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= 3(∬_(C)f(x, y)dA + ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA) \\\\\n&= 3(8 + (-5)) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129013 1074 "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}3f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =3(8+(-5)) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129014 1071 "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int_0^{1} \\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1} 9\\sqrt{y^3+1}\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129014 1071 "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_0^{1} \\int_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1 } 9\\sqrt{y^3+1}\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129014 1074 "![](images/10/1045aaad8025b20309b92477b4a1cf98e138064210c6fbc960c64ec26c9951df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad \\sqrt{x} \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt{x} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx=y^2.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq y^2 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/40/40cbaaff270daddf4b60115cc272fbc8b165b83b8823d19de2c19802617ebdb4.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1}9\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}9\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+1}[x\\,]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{y}^{2}({y}^{3}+1)^{1/2}\\,dy \\\\  & =3{[\\frac{2}{3}({y}^{3}+1)^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[2({y}^{3}+1)^{3/2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(2)^{3/2}-2 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129014 1074 "![](images/10/1045aaad8025b20309b92477b4a1cf98e138064210c6fbc960c64ec26c9951df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad \\sqrt{x} \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$, is\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$, is\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt{x} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=y^2\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq y^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/40/40cbaaff270daddf4b60115cc272fbc8b165b83b8823d19de2c19802617ebdb4.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1}9\\sqrt{y^{3} + 1}dydx &= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{y^{2}}9\\sqrt{y^{3} + 1}dxdy \\\\\n&= 9\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{y^{3} + 1}[\\int_{0}^{y^{2}}dx]dy \\\\\n&= 9\\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{y^{3} + 1}[x]_{0}^{y^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 3\\int_{0}^{1}3y^{2}(y^{3} + 1)^{1/2}dy \\\\\n&= 3[\\frac{2}{3}(y^{3} + 1)^{3/2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= [2(y^{3} + 1)^{3/2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 2(2)^{3/2} - 2 \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{2} - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129015 1071 "Change the order of integration in the double integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1\\displaystyle \\int_{2x}^{3-x} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129015 1071 "Change the order of integration in the double integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1\\int_{2x}^{3-x} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x .$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129015 1074 "![](images/47/47f6b583b2dd059ad8df49b0ca4374a964b4a28e91eb242a0dd3c7b8476fd26e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 2x \\leq y \\leq 3-x \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type II regions. \n\n![](images/35/35b4b57f26c7903189899ec284faa39bb70fb3a80397b772376358d4d86b3ec1.png)\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(y),$  is \n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{y}{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the right boundary of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=3-y.\n$$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\Big\\{ 