ditivity property of double integrals, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= ∬_(D_{1})f(x, y)dA + ∬_(D_{2})f(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{1}^{e}f(x, y)dxdy + \\int_{2}^{3}\\int_{e^{y - 2}}^{e}f(x, y)dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129020 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_{R} y \\cos{x} \\sin{z} \\: \\textrm{d}V$\nover the box $R = \\bigg\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\pi \\bigg\\}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129020 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iiint\\limits_{R} y \\cos{x} \\sin{z} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box $R = \\bigg\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\pi \\bigg\\}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129020 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∭_(R)y\\cos x\\sin zdV &= \\int_{0}^{π}\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{π/2}y\\cos x\\sin zdxdydz\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration\n\n$$\nR = \\left[0,\\pi/2\\right]\\times[0,1]\\times\\left[0,\\pi\\right]\n$$\n\nis a rectangular box, and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z)$, we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos xdx\\int_{0}^{1}ydy\\int_{0}^{π}\\sin zdz\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos xdx\\int_{0}^{1}ydy\\int_{0}^{π}\\sin zdz &= [\\sin x]_{0}^{π/2} \\cdot [\\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{1} \\cdot [- \\cos z]_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= (1 - 0) \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2} - 0) \\cdot (1 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129020 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R = \\left[0,\\pi/2\\right]\\times[0,1]\\times\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$ is a rectangular box, and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129022 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/8} \\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\sin2\\theta} r \\, \\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129022 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/8} \\int_0^{\\sin2\\theta} r \\, \\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129022 1074 "First, we integrate with respect to $r$, treating $\\theta$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/8}\\int_{0}^{\\sin 2θ}rdrdθ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{π/8}[r^{2}]_{0}^{\\sin 2θ}dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{π/8}\\sin^{2} 2θdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} = 1-2\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin^22\\theta = \\dfrac12\\left(1-\\cos4\\theta\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{π/8}\\sin^{2} 2θdθ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{π/8}\\frac{1}{2}(1 - \\cos 4θ)dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\int_{0}^{π/8}1 - \\cos 4θdθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[θ - \\frac{1}{4}\\sin 4θ]_{0}^{π/8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{π}{8} - \\frac{1}{4}\\sin (\\frac{π}{2})) - (0 - \\frac{1}{4}\\sin (0))] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{π}{8} - \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{π}{8} - \\frac{2}{8}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{32}(π - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129022 1074 "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{[{r}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} = 1-2\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin^22\\theta = \\dfrac12\\left(1-\\cos4\\theta\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(0-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{2}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}(\\pi -2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129023 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129023 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)y\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA &= ∬_(Δ)(r\\sin θ)\\sqrt{(r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2}}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∬_(Δ)r^{2}\\sin θ\\sqrt{r^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ\\sin^{2} θ)}drdθ \\\\\n&= ∬_(Δ)r^{3}\\sin θ\\sqrt{\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ}drdθ \\\\\n&= ∬_(Δ)r^{3}\\sin θdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\int_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\sin θdrdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129024 1071 "Integrate the function $f(x, y) = 15xy^2$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $x \\geq 0,y \\geq 0$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129024 1071 "Integrate the function $f(x, y) = 15xy^2$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $x \\geq 0,y \\geq 0.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129024 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/05/051cfbbf3fd37f01ff7e4442fccdc5ec4ce506e3c452b68bfa2ed5ae6e5cf6b9.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:0\\leq \\theta \\leq  \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}15x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}15r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({2}^{5}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32({sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{3}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =32(1-0) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129024 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D$:\n\n![](images/05/051cfbbf3fd37f01ff7e4442fccdc5ec4ce506e3c452b68bfa2ed5ae6e5cf6b9.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:0\\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)15xy^{2}dA &= ∬_(Δ)15r\\cos θ \\cdot (r\\sin θ)^{2}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\int_{0}^{2}15r^{4}\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/2}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θ\\int_{0}^{2}5r^{4}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/2}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θ[r^{5}]_{0}^{2}dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/2}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θ(2^{5} - 0)dθ \\\\\n&= 32\\int_{0}^{π/2}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdθ \\\\\n&= 32[\\sin^{3} θ]_{0}^{π/2} \\\\\n&= 32(\\sin^{3} (\\frac{π}{2}) - \\sin^{3} (0)) \\\\\n&= 32(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129025 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the probability mass function $f(y)$, shown in the table below, and the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$, compute $F(2)$.\n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(y)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ |" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129025 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the probability mass function $f(y),$ shown in the table below, and the cumulative distribution function $F(y),$ compute $F(2).$\n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(y)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ |" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129025 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $Y$ is the function $F(y)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(y) = P(Y \\leq y)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(2) &= P(Y \\le 2) \\\\\n&= P(Y ∈ {1, 2}) \\\\\n&= P(Y = 1) + P(Y = 2) \\\\\n&= f(1) + f(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129025 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $Y$ is the function $F(y)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(y) = P(Y \\leq y).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & =P(Y\\le 2) \\\\  & =P(Y\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =P(Y=1)+P(Y=2) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129026 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P\\left( 2 \\leq  Y <     5 \\right).$\n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(y)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{6}$ | $1$ |" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129026 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P\\left(2 \\leq Y < 5 \\right)$.\n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $F(y)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{6}$ | $1$ |" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129026 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $Y$ is the function $F(y)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(y) = P(Y \\leq y)\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability of a random variable $Y$ over an interval, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(a < X \\leq b) = F(b) - F(a)\n$$\n\nHere, we want to compute\n\n$$\nP\\left(2 \\leq Y < 5 \\right)\n$$\n\nHowever, this interval is stated in the form\n\n$$\na \\leq Y < b\n$$\n\nnot\n\n$$\na < Y \\leq b\n$$\n\nSo first, we must convert the interval\n\n$$\n2 \\leq Y < 5\n$$\n\nto the form\n\n$$\na < X \\leq b\n$$\n\nLooking at the given possible values of $Y$, we see that\n\n$$\n2 \\leq Y\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $1 < Y$, and $Y< 5$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\nY \\leq 4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(2 \\le Y < 5) &= P(1 < Y \\le 4) \\\\\n&= F(4) - F(1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186129026 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $Y$ is the function $F(y)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(y) = P(Y \\leq y).\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability of a random variable $Y$ over an interval, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(a <            X \\leq b) = F(b) - F(a).\n$$\n\nHere, we want to compute $P\\left( 2 \\leq  Y <     5 \\right).$ However, this interval is stated in the form $a \\leq  Y <    b,$ not $a <           Y \\leq b.$\n\nSo first, we must convert the interval $2 \\leq  Y <     5$ to the form $a <         X \\leq b.$ Looking at the given possible values of $Y,$ we see that $2 \\leq Y$ is equivalent to $1 <        Y,$ and  $Y<   5$ is equivalent to $Y \\leq 4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le Y<5) & =P(1<Y\\le 4) \\\\  & =F(4)-F(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186129027 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X=5)$.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ 