ollowing result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) < 0$, we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty, -2)$ and $(0,2)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (0,2)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133634 1071 "If ![](images/8d/8d9347b89864ea011c9dd3fbd17b94f05c479ff7a96343aae7928b241491c3ba.png) ![](images/b5/b5521921f5997e3cb748c518a9823574fcdfb82cd4bb1d1f7bbbf17d2f9ac06a.png) and $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 34,$ then what is $\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}}?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133634 1071 "If $\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 34$, then what is $\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}}$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133634 1074 "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}$, we compute $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ first and then multiply the result by $2$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot 2v &= 2(u \\cdot v) \\\\\n&= 2(u_{1} \\cdot v_{1} + u_{2} \\cdot v_{2} + u_{3} \\cdot v_{3} + u_{4} \\cdot v_{4}) \\\\\n&= 2(7 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 2 + (-9) \\cdot (-5) + 3 \\cdot (-2)) \\\\\n&= 2(7 + 10 + 45 - 6) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 56 \\\\\n&= 112\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $3\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$, we multiply $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ by $3$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3v \\cdot v &= 3(v \\cdot v) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 34 \\\\\n&= 102\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}} =\\dfrac{112}{102}=\\dfrac{56}{51}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133634 1074 "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot 2\\mathbf{v},$ we compute $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ first and then multiply the result by $2.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot 2v & =2(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =2({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =2(7\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2+(-9)\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2(7+10+45-6) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 56 \\\\  & =112.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $3\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we multiply $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ by $3.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3v\\cdot v & =3(v\\cdot v) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 34 \\\\  & =102.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}} =\\dfrac{112}{102}=\\dfrac{56}{51}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133635 1071 "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector ![](images/1e/1e710ca7b3b7bea626de36181a30869b0f114a4497843c6b6ea8b8966a91d208.png) under this inner product." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133635 1071 "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3$.\nCalculate the norm of the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133635 1074 "The norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+4+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133635 1074 "The norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖v‖ = \\sqrt{⟨v, v⟩}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖v‖ &= \\sqrt{⟨v, v⟩} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 3) + 2 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot (1 \\cdot 1)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{18 + 4 + 3} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133636 1071 "Given the vector ![](images/b8/b8e1e42d3c85f7b3783a0c46d5075e0c588f22a030f3d6a84da9ad3444ad35d0.png), which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $\\mathbf{u}$?\n![](images/6e/6e69c8002a518487ce4821efe1faae85ba7ec365b1bedf23d7bfcd30e10e42b5.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133636 1071 "Given the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $\\mathbf{u}$?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133636 1074 "We compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot v &= u_{1} \\cdot v_{1} + u_{2} \\cdot v_{2} + u_{3} \\cdot v_{3} + u_{4} \\cdot v_{4} \\\\\n&= (-5) \\cdot 1 + (-3) \\cdot (-3) + 2 \\cdot (-4) + 0 \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&=-5 + 9 - 8 + 0 \\\\\n&=-4.\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot w &= u_{1} \\cdot w_{1} + u_{2} \