c paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=2x$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x - 1)^2 +  y^2 = 1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\n![](images/2c/2c16cae9e18867b3fa0d61203a6a91b9944e6595fa15bfd8d3b424346fefabee.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $y=-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}$ and bounded *above* by $y=\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}}_{g_1(x)} \\leq y \\leq \\underbrace{\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}}_{g_2(x)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=0$ and bounded *above* by $x=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{0}_a \\leq x \\leq \\underbrace{2}_b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $R$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2,  \\:\\:-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2} \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}, \\:\\: x^2+y^2 \\leq z \\leq 2x\\right\\}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133642 1074 "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\nLet's find a type I representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces $f_1(x,y)$ and $f_2(x,y)$. Since all $z$ in the region are bounded *below* by\n\n$$\nz=x^2+y^2\n$$\n\nand *above* by $z=2x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+y^2}_{f_1(x,y)} \\leq z \\leq \\underbrace{2x}_{f_2(x,y)}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid\n\n$$\nz=x^2+y^2\n$$\n\nand the plane $z=2x$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already s