y the lines $x=0$, $y=0$, and the curve $y=\\sqrt{4 - x^2}$. What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133654 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $x=0,$ $y=0,$ and the curve $y=\\sqrt{4 - x^2}.$ What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133654 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/3d/3d67d73d3ede011d6c2fe4bc9e160aa3c2af522bff6cddfcc7c351c97418e764.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x  \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt{4-x^2} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}(x+y)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133654 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/3d/3d67d73d3ede011d6c2fe4bc9e160aa3c2af522bff6cddfcc7c351c97418e764.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt{4-x^2} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(x + y)dA &= \\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}(x + y)dydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133655 1071 "If $D$ is the finite region in the first quadrant enclosed by the curves $y=3x$ and $y=x^3$, then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left({x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133655 1071 "If $D$ is the finite region in the first quadrant enclosed by the curves $y=3x$ and $y=x^3,$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left( {x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133655 1074 "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/f8/f8a5fd7fc3e98d7f08b5ea48636e5de4a1a45b7a94fbe9e968789afcfe3a582c.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region.\n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x$:\n$x^{3}|= 3x; x^{3} - 3x|= 0; x(x^{2} - 3)|= 0$\nSo, $x$-limits are $0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{3}$. Note that we disregarded the solution $x=-\\sqrt 3$ since $x\\geq 0$ in our region.\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/23/232f885d2230694bf62f81f5987cd2b3a12a49430d90d2cae4712c552587a48c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{3}, \\quad x^3 \\leq y \\leq 3x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\int_{x^{3}}^{3x}f(x, y)dydx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}[\\int_{x^{3}}^{3x}f(x, y)dy]dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133655 1074 "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/f8/f8a5fd7fc3e98d7f08b5ea48636e5de4a1a45b7a94fbe9e968789afcfe3a582c.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =3x \\\\ {x}^{3}-3x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are $0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{3}.$ Note that we disregarded the solution $x=-\\sqrt 3$ since $x\\geq 0$ in our region.\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/23/232f885d2230694bf62f81f5987cd2b3a12a49430d90d2cae4712c552587a48c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x  \\leq \\sqrt{3}, \\quad x^3 \\leq y \\leq 3x \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{3x}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}[{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{3x}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133656 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\displaystyle \\int_0^x x^2y \\,\\textrm{d}y \\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133656 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_0^x x^2y \\,\\textrm{d}y \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133656 1074 "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x}x^{2}ydydx &= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2}[\\int_{0}^{x}ydy]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2}[\\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{x}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2}(\\frac{x^{2}}{2} - 0)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx &= \\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{x^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133656 1074 "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133657 1071 "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region below the $x$-axis enclosed by the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133657 1071 "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region below the $x$-axis enclosed by the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133657 1074 "Since\n\n$$\ny \\leq 0\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 4 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y = -\\sqrt{4-x^2}\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/31/31e7bb281a36a3676730de0883d7abd5445093713de1bbd41899cce50a7e0e48.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the circle with the line $y=0$.\nSo, the $x$-limits are\n$-2 \\le x \\le 2$.\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/2a/2a84d17b83fb1e2036c6edc0a71c5c10a58bf893c33bbf38662ebb417aab3164.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region:\n\n$$\nD = \\Big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad -\\sqrt{4-x^2} \\leq y \\leq 0 \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{-2}^2 \\int_{-\\sqrt{4-x^2}}^{0} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-2}^{2}\\int_{-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}^{0}xydydx &= \\int_{-2}^{2}x[\\int_{-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}^{0}ydy]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-2}^{2}x[\\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}^{0}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}x(0 - (-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}})^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}x(- (4 - x^{2}))dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}x(x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}(x^{3} - 4x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}(x^{3} - 4x)dx &= \\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{x^{4}}{4} - 2x^{2}]_{-2}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}((4 - 8) - (4 - 8)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133657 1074 "Since $y \\leq 0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 4\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny = -\\sqrt{4-x^2}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/31/31e7bb281a36a3676730de0883d7abd5445093713de1bbd41899cce50a7e0e48.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the circle with the line $y=0.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$-2 \\le x \\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/2a/2a84d17b83fb1e2036c6edc0a71c5c10a58bf893c33bbf38662ebb417aab3164.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD = \\Big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad -\\sqrt{4-x^2} \\leq y \\leq 0 \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{-2}^2 \\int_{-\\sqrt{4-x^2}}^{0} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}^{0}xy\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}x[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}^{0}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}^{0}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x(0-(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})