-2x$ in terms of $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y = x^{2} & ⟹x = \\sqrt{y} \\\\ y = 3 - 2x & ⟹x = \\frac{3 - y}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y$:\n$\\sqrt{y}|= \\frac{3 - y}{2}; 4y|= (3 - y)^{2}; 4y|= 9 - 6y + y^{2}; y^{2} - 10y + 9|= 0; (y - 1)(y - 9)|= 0$So, since our region lies below the line $y=3$, the $y$-limits are\n$1 \\le y \\le 3$.\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/3c/3c56798a33ee2da6d928174b67546945be4bf3594d15e89f3ca39119dd4e2c62.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 3, \\quad \\dfrac{3-y}{2} \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= \\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{(3 - y)/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x, y)dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133660 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/ff/ffbe0db1ae1bb082e63b0d97324b43dd78334dfbd1e88aaa0744be039dd0efc6.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y=3-2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{3-y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{3-y}{2} \\\\ 4y & =(3-y)^{2} \\\\ 4y & =9-6y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-10y+9 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies below the line $y=3,$ the $y$-limits are \n\n$$\n1 \\le y \\le 3.\n$$\n\n- To determine the left an