nt }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}({y}^{2}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}y\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}]}_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133662 1071 "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x=0$, and above the line $y=-1$, as shown in the picture.\n\n![](images/f9/f93aeee59ad97fde0efceff342e32a19062187261abec865616bfa963f1084a6.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186133662 1071 "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x=0,$ and above the line $y=-1,$ as shown in the picture.\n\n![](images/f9/f93aeee59ad97fde0efceff342e32a19062187261abec865616bfa963f1084a6.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186133662 1074 "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since\n\n$$\nx \\ge 0\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following equations in terms of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + y^{2} = 4 & ⟹x = \\sqrt{4 - y^{2}} \\\\ x = 0 & ⟹x = 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$