h of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- The planes intersect on a straight line." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147349 1071 "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}$.\n2. The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n3. The planes intersect on a straight line." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147349 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle$.\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n$n_{1} \\cdot n_{2}|= ⟨4,-2, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 2, 1, 0⟩; n|= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}0; - 2 \\mid 1 \\mid 0; = 0$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However, the first plane passes through the origin $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: $-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0$ So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147349 1074 "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨4,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ n & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However,  the first plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147350 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y'' + 9y = 6\\cos x$.\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\cos(3x)$ and $y_2 = \\sin(3x)$.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution\n$y_p(x) = u_1(x) y_1 + u_2(x) y_2$\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n${u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = 6\\cos x; u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle u_2(x) = \\displaystyle \\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x$, find the function $g$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147350 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 9y = 6\\cos x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\cos(3x)$ and $y_2 = \\sin(3x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x) y_1 + u_2(x) y_2\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle u_2(x) = \\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x,$ find the function $g.$