os x \\cos(3x)\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147350 1074 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 6\\cos x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)+3{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))}^{′}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2.$ We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147351 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\n1. $y'' + 81y = 0$\n2. $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n3. $y'' - 81y = 0$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147351 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\n- $y'' + 81y = 0$\n\n- $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n\n- $y'' - 81y = 0$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147351 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I takes the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nwith\n$\\omega^2 = 81 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 9$.\nTherefore, equation I represents SHM.\n- Equation II does not take the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nbecause it has a $y'$ term.\n- Equation III does not take the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$.\nIt would require $\\omega^2 = -81$, which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147351 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I takes the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 81 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I represents SHM.\n\n- Equation II does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nbecause it has a $y'$ term.\n\n- Equation III does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt would require $\\omega^2 = -81,$ which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147352 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 100y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147352 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 100y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147352 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere the period $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 100\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 10\n$$\n\nso the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147352 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere the period $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 100\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 10,\n$$\n\nso the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\,\\textrm{s}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147353 1071 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 9 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n\n- $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n\n- $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147353 1071 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 9 y = 0$.\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n2. $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n3. $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147353 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1. $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n2. $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n3. $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n1. $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n2. $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n3. $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147353 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147354 1071 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 36y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$, find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147354 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 36y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\textrm{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147354 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nand the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)}\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 36\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 6\n$$\n\nso the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(6t+\\phi)}\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0) = 0$. Substituting this into the equation above gives\n\n$$\nR\\sin\\phi = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\phi = 0\n$$\n\nSince $\\phi=0$, our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{6 t}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0$, which means that $y'(0) = 3$. Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n