194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147355 1071 "A particle of mass $36\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is $y'' + \\dfrac{1}{2}y = 0$\n\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi}\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}$\n\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)},$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147355 1074 "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m=36$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k=9$ has\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{k}{m} = \\dfrac{9}{36} = \\dfrac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nso its equation of motion is \n\n$$\ny'' + \\dfrac{1}{4}y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is different from the equation in statement I.\n\n- Statement II is true. The frequency $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi} \\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\\,R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1}),\\,R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting $\\omega = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\phi_1 = \\phi$ in the form $R\\sin{(\\omega t+\\phi_2)}$ gives\n\n$$\ny(t) = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147355 1074 "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m=36$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k=9$ has\n$\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{k}{m} = \\dfrac{9}{36} = \\dfrac{1}{4}$,\nso its equation of motion is\n$y'' + \\dfrac{1}{4}y = 0$,\nwhich is different from the equation in statement I.\n- Statement II is true. The frequency $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) is given by\n$f = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi} \\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- Statement III is true. The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms:\n$A\\cos ωt + B\\sin ωt, R\\cos (ωt + ϕ_{1}), R\\sin (ωt + ϕ_{2})$.\nWriting $\\omega = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\phi_1 = \\phi$ in the form $R\\sin{(\\omega t+\\phi_2)}$ gives\n$y(t) = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147356 1071 "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?\n\n- $-2x^2y''+6xy'-5y=0$\n\n- $xy''+2y'-3xy=0$\n\n- $y''-3x^2y=\\sqrt{x}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147356 1071 "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?\n\n1. $-2x^2y''+6xy'-5y=0$\n2. $xy''+2y'-3xy=0$\n3. $y''-3x^2y=\\sqrt{x}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147356 1074 "The second-order Cauchy-Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\nax^2y''+bxy'+cy=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$, and $c$ are constants. With that in mind, let's inspect each of the given equations.\n\n- Equation I is a Cauchy-Euler equation with $a=-2$, $b=6$, $c=-5$, and $f(x)=0$.\n- Equation II is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ is not multiplied by an $x^2$. Instead, it is multiplied by an $x$.\n- Equation III is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ term is not multiplied by an $x^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147356 1074 "The second-order Cauchy-Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\nax^2y''+bxy'+cy=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants. With that in mind, let's inspect each of the given equations.\n\n- Equation I is a Cauchy-Euler equation with $a=-2,$ $b=6,$ $c=-5,$ and $f(x)=0.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ is not multiplied by an $x^2.$ Instead, it is multiplied by an $x.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ term is not multiplied by an $x^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147357 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n3t^2 y''+8ty' -2y = 0, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147357 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$3t^2 y''+8ty' -2y = 0, \\quad t\\gt 0$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147357 1074 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=t^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3t^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)t^{λ - 2} + 8t \\cdot λt^{λ - 1} - 2t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\n3λ(λ - 1)t^{λ} + 8λt^{λ} - 2t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nt^{λ}[3λ(λ - 1) + 8λ - 2] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3λ(λ - 1) + 8λ - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n3λ^{2} + 5λ - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 2)(3λ - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= c_{1}t^{-2} + c_{2}t^{1/3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{c_{1}}{t^{2}} + c_{2}t^{1/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147357 1074 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=t^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+8t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 3\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+8\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[3\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -2] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ 3{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(3\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = -2$ and $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={c}_{1}{t}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}+{c}_{2}{t}^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147358 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{c_1}{x^3}+ c_2x^{2},\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0,  \\quad y'(1) = 5, \\quad x \\gt 0.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147358 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$x^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y=\\dfrac{c_1}{x^3}+ c_2x^{2}$,\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$x^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0, \\quad y'(1) = 5, \\quad x \\gt 0$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147358 1074 "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0$. Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0 = c_1+c_2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad c_2 = -c_1$.\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 5$. Differentiating $y$, we get\n$y^{′}|=-3\\frac{c_{1}}{x^{4}} + 2c_{2}x$.\nSubstituting $y'(1)=5$ and $c_2=-c_1$ into the above gives\n$5|=-3c_{1} + 2c_{2}; 5|=-3c_{1} + 2(-c_{1}); 5|=-5c_{1}; c_{1}|=-1⇒c_{2} = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x^{2}-\\dfrac{1}{x^3}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147358 1074 "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0.$ Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0 = c_1+c_2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad  c_2 = -c_1.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 5.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-3\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{4}}+2{c}_{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y'(1)=5$ and $c_2=-c_1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =-3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ 5 & =-3{c}_{1}+2(-{c}_{1}) \\\\ 5 & =-5{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =-1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x^{2}-\\dfrac{1}{x^3}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147359 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n2x^2 y'' + 6 xy' + 2y = 0, \\qquad x>0.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147359 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$2x^2 y'' + 6 xy' + 2y = 0, \\qquad x>0$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147359 1074 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=x^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)x^{λ - 2} + 6x \\cdot λx^{λ 