ving for ![](images/fc/fc1f5e662d05224ca25f4893c62389cc5d8b11acbae73f269a7921e097374b85.png) gives\n![](images/68/683610152cf39c63fcf62ccdeadf1beed4ff8ef1cfb6a28452420974901cdd0c.png)\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation is\n\n![](images/7c/7ce940ebe652683318187b453d18127a4334b5c0dfef68da5e9a74e937f2d734.png)\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index equals ![](images/00/0021ff77f0c4565f235da704e0b4d45d1145486e46d721ea834cba941061b020.png) since ![](images/ea/ea7eeb2aa3909170b1ed83f408f8b17ec17d5012db54fd49910e85034899dbb8.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147377 1074 "First, we find $y'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(x) &= (∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= (a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y'(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} + 4x∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} + ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)4a_{n}x^{n + 1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-1$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+1) a_{m+1} x^{m} + \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty 4a_n x^{n+1} =0\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing\n\n$$\nm = n+1\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+1) a_{m+1} x^{m} + \\sum_{m=1}^\\infty 4a_{m-1} x^{m} =0\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (n+1) a_{n+1} x^{n} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty 4 a_{n-1} x^{n} =0\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n$. So, we take out the term $n = 0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\na_1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (n+1) a_{n+1} x^{n} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty 4a_{n-1} x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(n + 1)a_{n + 1}x^{n} + 4a_{n - 1}x^{n}] &= 0 \\\\\na_{1} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(n + 1)a_{n + 1} + 4a_{n - 1}]x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of $x^n$, we obtain\n\n$$\na_1 = 0, \\qquad (n+1) a_{n+1} = -4a_{n-1}\n$$\n\nSolving for $a_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = -\\dfrac{4a_{n-1}}{n+1}, \\quad n=1,2,3 \\ldots\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = -\\dfrac{4a_{n-1}}{n+1}, \\quad a_1=0, \\quad n=1,2,3 \\ldots\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index equals to $0$, since $a_1=0$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147378 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?\n\n1. $y''+4y' - y = 0$\n2. $y''+ 4 = 0$\n3. $y''+y'+4y = 0$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147378 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?\n\n- $y''+4y' - y = 0$\n\n- $y''+ 4 = 0$\n\n- $y''+y'+4y = 0$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147378 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I does not take the form\n$y'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0$.\nIt would require $\\omega^2=-1$, which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0$.\n- Equation II does not take the form\n$y'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nbecause it has a constant term, $4$.\n- Equation III takes the form\n$y'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nwith\n$\\omega^2 = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 2$\nand $b = 1$. Therefore, equation III represents damped harmonic motion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147378 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt would require $\\omega^2=-1,$ which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0.$\n\n- Equation II does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nbecause it has a constant term, $4.$\n\n- Equation III takes the form\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 2\n$$\n\nand $b = 1.$ Therefore, equation III represents damped harmonic motion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147379 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n\n- $y'' +2y'+ y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\n- $y'' +4y'+3y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147379 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n2. $y'' +2y'+ y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n3. $y'' +4y'+3y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147379 1074 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} > 0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} = 0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} < 0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda + 2 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={1}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =-7<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to light damping. Therefore, statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +2y'+y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 +2\\lambda + 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(1) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to critical damping. Therefore, statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +4y'+3 y$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 +4\\lambda + 3 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =4>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to heavy damping. Therefore, statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147379 1074 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} > 0$ leads to heavy damping,\n- $\\mathcal{D} = 0$ leads to critical damping, and\n- $\\mathcal{D} < 0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n$\\lambda^2 + \\lambda + 2 = 0$.\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is:\n$D = 1^{2} - 4(1)(2)|=-7 < 0$,\nwhich corresponds to light damping. Therefore, statement I is correct.\n- The differential equation $y'' +2y'+y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n$\\lambda^2 +2\\lambda + 1 = 0$.\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n$D = 2^{2} - 4(1)(1)|= 0$,\nwhich corresponds to critical damping. Therefore, statement II is correct.\n- The differential equation $y'' +4y'+3 y$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n$\\lambda^2 +4\\lambda + 3 = 0$.\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n$D = 4^{2} - 4(1)(3)|= 4 > 0$,\nwhich corresponds to heavy damping. Therefore, statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147380 1071 "A particle of mass $5\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 15\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2$. The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c = 10\\, \\textrm{kg/s}$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147380 1071 "A particle of mass $5\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 15\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c = 10\\, \\textrm{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147380 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$, $k$, and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n5 y'' =-10y' -15 y\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5y^{″} + 10y^{′} + 15y &= 0 \\\\\ny^{″} + 2y^{′} + 3y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147380 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y .\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n5 y'' =-10y' -15 y .\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligne