 can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny_c =e^{-t}\\left(A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $\\Omega = 2\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\Omega}\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi \\,\\textrm{s}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147382 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0$. Determine whether each equation is forced or free, and whether it is damped.\n\n1. $y''+y'+3y = t$\n2. $y''+ 4y = e^t$\n3. $y''+y'+3y = 0$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186147382 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0.$ Determine whether each equation is forced or free, and whether it is damped.\n\n- $y''+y'+3y = t$\n\n- $y''+ 4y = e^t$\n\n- $y''+y'+3y = 0$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186147382 1074 "The differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = f(t)\n$$\n\nis forced if $f(t)$ is not identical to zero, and damped if $b>0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify each equation.\n\n- Equation I represents forced harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=t$ is not always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0$.\n- Equation II represents forced harmonic motion without damping. The forcing function $f(t)=e^t$ is not always zero, but there is no damping term since $b=0$.\n- Equation III represents free (non-forced) harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=0$ is always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [1759218