 integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-(0-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151956 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,\n$$\n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ = \\cos θ, \\\\\ny &= r\\sin θ = \\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\n, where\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ) &= \\frac{\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ}{\\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\csc^{2} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dr}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(1) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ = \\sqrt{1^{2} + 0^{2}} = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_(C)\\frac{x^{2} + y^{2}}{y^{2}}ds &= \\int_{π/6}^{π/2}f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π/6}^{π/2}\\csc^{2} θ \\cdot 1 \\cdot dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π/6}^{π/2}\\csc^{2} θdθ \\\\\n&= \\left.- \\cot θ