n$$\ny=\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\n, where\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = xy(1-x^2)\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ) &= \\cos^{2} θ \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin θ(1 - (\\cos^{2} θ)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dr}{dθ} = \\frac{d}{dθ}(\\cos θ) =-\\sin θ\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ &= \\sqrt{(\\cos θ)^{2} + (-\\sin θ)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_(C)xy(1 - x^{2})ds &= \\int_{0}^{π/2}f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ) \\cdot 1 \\cdot dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ)dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ)dθ &= \\int_{1}^{0} - u^{3}(1 - u^{4})du \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(u^{3} - u^{7})du \\\\\n&= [\\frac{u^{4}}{4} - \\frac{u^{8}}{8}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{8}) - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151964 1071 "If ![](images/51/518a42d25cd993f4bca2498bd9e91a466c7f0c4d71e109b6ab95c9717d34e9e0.png) find $\\,\\overline{\\mathbf{x}}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151964 1071 "If $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 + 5\\textrm{i} \\\\ 5 - 9\\textrm{i} \\\\ -7 + 8\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ find $\\,\\overline{\\mathbf{x}}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151964 1074 "To find the complex conjugate of a vector, we take the complex conjugate of the entries of the vector. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 + 5\\textrm{i} \\\\ 5 - 9\\textrm