=7$ and $d=-2,$ which implies that\n\n$$\nab+cd=(-3) \\cdot 5 + 7 \\cdot (-2) = -29.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151965 1074 "For the given vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 + 7\\textrm{i} \\\\ 5 - 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 + 7\\textrm{i} \\\\ 5 - 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(Re(x)) + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(Im(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\na=-3, b=5, c=7\n$$\n\nand $d=-2$, which implies that\n\n$$\nab+cd=(-3) \\cdot 5 + 7 \\cdot (-2) = -29\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151966 1071 "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n- $\\, (2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\n\n- $\\,y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\n\n- $\\,y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151966 1071 "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n1. $\\, (2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\n2. $\\,y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\n3. $\\,y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151966 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x$. If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n$(2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\nis homogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 0$ for all $x$.\n- The equation\n$y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\nis inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 3e^x$, which is different from zero.\n- Writing the equation\n$y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$\nin the form $ay'' + by' + cy = f$, we have\n$y'' + x^3 y = 8$.\nThis equation is inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 8$, which is different from zero." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151966 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0\n$$\n\nis homogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x\n$$\n\nis inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 3e^x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0\n$$\n\nin the form $ay'' + by' + cy = f,$ we have\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y = 8.\n$$\n\nThis equation is inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 8,$ which is different from zero." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151967 1071 "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\n\n- $y''+y'=x \\cos x$\n\n- $yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151967 1071 "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n1. $y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\n2. $y''+y'=x \\cos x$\n3. $yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151967 1074 "A second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n$y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $(y-1)y'$.\n\n- The equation\n$y''+y'=x \\cos x$\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x) = 1, b(x) = 1, c(x) = 0$, and $f(x) = x\\cos{x}$.\n- The equation\n$yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $yy''$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151967 1074 "A second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $(y-1)y'.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny''+y'=x \\cos x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x) = 1, b(x) = 1, c(x) = 0,$ and $f(x) = x\\cos{x}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nyy'' - y'+ 9 = 0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $yy''.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151968 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2y'' + xy' = x+2$\n\n- $9y'' + 4y = e^x, \\quad y(0) = 1,  \\quad  y'(4)= 1$\n\n- $y''+xy'+x^2y= \\sin x, \\quad y(1) = 0,  \\quad  y'(1)= 5$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151968 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n1. $x^2y'' + xy' = x+2$\n2. $9y'' + 4y = e^x, \\quad y(0) = 1, \\quad y'(4)= 1$\n3. $y''+xy'+x^2y= \\sin x, \\quad y(1) = 0, \\quad y'(1)= 5$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151968 1074 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y$, then this is an **initial value problem.**\n\nWith this in mind, let's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- System I is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n- System II is not an initial value problem because it consists of a differential equation together with two conditions in *different* locations, $x=0$ and $x=4$. So this is a **boundary value problem**, not an initial value problem.\n- System III is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151968 1074 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem.**\n\nWith this in mind, let's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- System I is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- System II is not an initial value problem because it consists of a differential equation together with two conditions in *different* locations, $x=0$ and $x=4.$ So this is a **boundary value problem**, not an initial value problem.\n\n- System III is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151969 1071 "For the differential equation $2xy'' +y' - y = \\cos x$, what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151969 1071 "For the differential equation $2xy'' +y' - y = \\cos x,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151969 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n2xy'' +y' - y = 0\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151969 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n2xy'' +y' - y = 0.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151970 1071 "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation $y'' - 2y - 3y = 0$, which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$\n2. $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$\n3. $y(x) =-e^{x} + e^{3x}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151970 1071 "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation $y'' - 2y - 3y = 0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$\n\n- $y(x) =- e^{x} + e^{3x}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151970 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c_1$ and $c_2$ are constants, then\n\n$$\ny(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x)\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{-x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny_2(x) =e^{3x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = c_1 e^{-x} + c_2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, $c_1 = 7$ and $c_2 = -6$.\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation. The superposition principle only applies to linear combinations of known solutions. It does not extend to the divisions of the known solutions.\n- The function $y(x) =-e^{x} + e^{3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation because $e^x$ is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151970 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c_1$ and $c_2$ are constants, then $y(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) =e^{3x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = c_1 e^{-x} + c_2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, $c_1 = 7$ and $c_2 = -6.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation. The superposition principle only applies to linear combinations of known solutions. It does not extend to the divisions of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = - e^{x} + e^{3x}$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation because $e^x$ is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151971 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' + xy' + 4y = 0,\\quad x > 0.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151971 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$x^2 y'' + xy' + 4y = 0,\\quad x > 0$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151971 1074 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=x^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)x^{λ - 2} + x \\cdot λx^{λ - 1} + 4x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nλ(λ - 1)x^{λ} + λx^{λ} + 4x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{λ}[λ(λ - 1) + λ + 4] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ(λ - 1) + λ + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nλ &= ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=c_1 \\cos(2 \\ln{x}) + c_2\\sin(2 \\ln{x})\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151971 1074 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=x^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $