into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u)-10{e}^{5x}({u}^{′}+5u)+25{e}^{5x}u & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u-10{u}^{′}-50u+25u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{5x}\\neq 0$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu(x) = c_1x+c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = ue^{5x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\left(c_1 x + c_2\\right).\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\cdot x = xe^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = e^{10x} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny=Ae^{5x}+Bxe^{5x}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151989 1071 "Solve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' + y = 0, \\quad y(\\pi) = 2, \\quad y'(\\pi) = -1\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151989 1071 "Solve the following initial value problem:\n\n$y'' + y = 0, \\quad y(\\pi) = 2, \\quad y'(\\pi) = -1$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151989 1074 "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= -1 \\\\\nλ &= ± \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A \\cos x + B \\sin x\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find constants $A$ and $B$. To do this, we apply the initial conditions $y(\\pi)=2$, $y'(\\pi)=-1$.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\pi) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n$y(π)|= 2; A\\cos π + B\\sin π|= 2; - A|= 2; A|=-2$.\n- Computing $y'(x)$, we get\n$y' =-A \\sin{x} + B\\cos{x}$,\nand substituting $y'(\\pi)=-1$, we get\n$y^{′}(π)|=-1; - A\\sin π + B\\cos π|=-1; - B|=-1; B|= 1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -2\\cos{x} + \\sin{x}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151989 1074 "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A \\cos x + B \\sin x\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find constants $A$ and $B.$ To do this, we apply the initial conditions $y(\\pi)=2,$ $y'(\\pi)=-1.$\n\n- Substituting $y(\\pi) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =2 \\\\ A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  & =2 \\\\ -A & =2 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing $y'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\ny' =  - A \\sin{x} + B\\cos{x},\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(\\pi)=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-1 \\\\ -A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  & =-1 \\\\ -B & =-1 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -2\\cos{x} + \\sin{x}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151990 1071 "Consider the differential equation $y'' - 3y'+ 2y=0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_1(x) = e^{x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n2. $y_2(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n3. The general solution of the equation is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151990 1071 "Consider the differential equation $y'' - 3y'+ 2y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x) = e^{x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- $y_2(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151990 1074 "Let's check each statement.\n\n- To determine whether $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y_1$ and its derivatives into the equation. Computing the first and second derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\ne^{x}  - 3e^{x} + 2e^{x} = 0. \\color{green}\\quad\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_1$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of $y_2$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n4e^{2x}  - 3(2e^{2x})  + 2(e^{2x} ) = 0. \\quad\\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_2$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- Both $y_1=e^x$ and $y_2=e^{2x}$ are solutions of the equation. So, the general solution is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$ if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. To check if they are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{x})}^{′}{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian does not equal $0,$ the functions are linearly independent. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct statements are I, II, and III." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151990 1074 "Let's check each statement.\n\n- To determine whether $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y_1$ and its derivatives into the equation. Computing the first and second derivatives, we get\n$y_{1}^{′}(x)|= e^{x}; y_{1}^{″}(x)|= e^{x}$,\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n$e^{x} - 3e^{x} + 2e^{x} = 0. \\color{green}\\quad\\checkmark$\nSo $y_1$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of $y_2$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives, we get\n$y_{2}^{′}(x)|= 2e^{2x}; y_{2}^{″}(x)|= 4e^{2x}$,\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n$4e^{2x} - 3(2e^{2x}) + 2(e^{2x}) = 0. \\quad\\color{green}\\checkmark$\nSo $y_2$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n- Both $y_1=e^x$ and $y_2=e^{2x}$ are solutions of the equation. So, the general solution is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$ if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. To check if they are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n$W(y_{1}, y_{2})|=\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}; = y_{1}y_{2}^{′} - y_{1}^{′}y_{2}; = e^{x}(e^{2x})^{′} - (e^{x})^{′}e^{2x}; = 2e^{3x} - e^{3x}; = e^{3x}$.\nSince the Wronskian does not equal $0$, the functions are linearly independent. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct statements are I, II, and III." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151991 1071 "Given that $y_1(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation $x^2 y'' + 3xy' + y = 0$ for $x>0$, use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151991 1071 "Given that $y_1(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation $x^2 y'' + 3xy' + y = 0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151991 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2}(x) &= u(x)y_{1}(x) \\\\\n&= u(x) \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\\n&= (u(x))/(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating\n\n$$\ny_{2} = \\dfrac{u(x)}{x}\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2} &= \\frac{u}{x} \\\\\ny_{2}^{′} &= \\frac{u^{′}x - u}{x^{2}} \\\\\ny_{2}^{″} &= \\frac{u^{″}x^{2} - 2u^{′}x + 2u}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}y^{″} + 3xy^{′} + y &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2}(\\frac{u^{″}x^{2} - 2u^{′}x + 2u}{x^{3}}) + 3x(\\frac{u^{′}x - u}{x^{2}}) + \\frac{u}{x} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{u^{″}x^{2} - 2u^{′}x + 2u + 3u^{′}x - 3u + u}{x} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{u^{″}x^{2} + u^{′}x}{x} &= 0 \\\\\nu^{″}x + u^{′} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $w = u'$. Then our equation becomes a first-order equation:\n\n$$\nw' x + w = 0\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{c_1}{x}\n$$\n\nSince $w = u'$, we have\n\n$$\nu' = \\dfrac{c_1}{x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = c_1\\ln{x} + c_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{u}{x}\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{c_1\\ln{x} + c_2}{x}\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$, we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{x}\n$$\n\nFinally, because\n\n$$\nW(y_1, y_2) = \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{A}{x} + \\dfrac{B\\ln{x}}{x} = \\dfrac{A + B\\ln{x}}{x}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151991 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{u(x)}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y_{2} = \\dfrac{u(x)}{x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =\\frac{u}{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & =\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}})+3x(\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u+3{u}^{′}x-3u+u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}+{u}^{′}x}{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}x+{u}^{′} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation:\n\n$$\nw' x + w = 0\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{c_1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = \\dfrac{c_1}{x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = c_1\\ln{x} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = \\dfrac{u}{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{c_1\\ln{x} + c_2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{A}{x} + \\dfrac{B\\ln{x}}{x} = \\dfrac{A + B\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151995 1071 "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1$, as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)$?\n\n![](images/5d/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151995 1071 "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](images/5d/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151995 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0)$.\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151995 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151996 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151996 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n$x= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151996 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta$. We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 4\\cos θ \\\\\n\\frac{x}{4} &= \\cos θ \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4^{2}} &= \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{16} &= \\cos^{2} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny + 1 &= 2\\sin θ \\\\\n\\frac{y + 1}{2} &= \\sin θ \\\\\n((y + 1)^{2})/(2^{2}) &= \\sin^{2} θ \\\\\n((y + 1)^{2})/(4) &= \\sin^{2} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{16} + ((y + 1)^{2})/(4) &= \\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151996 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151997 1071 "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4$, as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)$?\n\n![](images/47/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151997 1071 "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](images/47/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151997 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k)$.\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2)$, $a=6$ and $b=4$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151997 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151998 1071 "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$?\n\n![](images/6c/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151998 1071 "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](images/6c/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186151998 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/a6/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to\n\n$$\n\\left[0, \\pi\\right]\n$$\n\nwe get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right]\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186151998 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/a6/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186152000 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0,\n\\ \\ y(1) = e, \\ \\ y'(1) = e ^{-2}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186152000 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$y'' + y - 2y =0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y = Ae^{x} + Be^{-2 x}$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n$y'' + y - 2y =0, \\ \\ y(1) = e, \\ \\ y'(1) = e ^{-2}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186152000 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =A{e}^{1}+B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ e & =Ae+B{e}^{-2} \\\\ A & =1-B{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =A{e}^{1}-2B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & =Ae-2B{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A=1-B{e}^{-3} \\\\ {e}^{-2}=Ae-2B{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A = 1 - B e ^{-3}$ in the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =(1-B{e}^{-3})e-2B{e}^{-2} \\\\ 1 & ={e}^{3}-B-2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $B= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3$ back into the equation $A = 1-Be^{-3}$ gives\n\n$$\nA = 1 -  \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 =   \\dfrac {2 +  e ^ {-3}} 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac {  e ^ {-3} + 2} 3  e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186152000 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n$e|= Ae^{1} + Be^{-2(1)}; e|= Ae + Be^{-2}; A|= 1 - Be^{-3}$.\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2}$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-x}$.\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n$e^{-2}|= Ae^{1} - 2Be^{-2(1)}; e^{-2}|= Ae - 2Be^{-2}$.\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{A = 1 - Be^{-3}; e^{-2} = Ae - 2Be^{-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nA = 1 - B e ^{-3}\n$$\n\nin the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{-2} &= (1 - Be^{-3})e - 2Be^{-2} \\\\\n1 &= e^{3} - B - 2B \\\\\nB &= \\frac{e^{3} - 1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting\n\n$$\nB= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3\n$$\n\nback into the equation\n\n$$\nA = 1-Be^{-3}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\nA = 1 - \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 = \\dfrac {2 + e ^ {-3}} 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac {e ^ {-3} + 2} 3 e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186152001 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 4 y' + 4y =0,\n\\qquad y(0) =  3, \\qquad y'(0) = 2.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186152001 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$y'' - 4 y' + 4y =0, \\qquad y(0) = 3, \\qquad y'(0) = 2$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186152001 1074 "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 4λ + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 2)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 2\n$$\n\nso we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A e ^ {2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=3$ into our general solution gives\n$3|= Ae^{2(0)} + B(0)e^{2(0)}; 3|= Ae^{0} + 0; 3|= A$.\n- To apply the condition $y' (0) = 2$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y' = 2 A e ^ {2 x} + B e ^ {2 x} + 2 B x e ^{2 x}$.\nThen, we substitute $y'(0)=2$ into the above to get\n$2|= 2Ae^{2(0)} + Be^{2(0)} + 2B(0)e^{2(0)}; 2|= 2Ae^{0} + Be^{0} + 0; 2|= 2A + B$.\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{3 = A; 2 = 2A + B\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=3$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= 6 + B \\\\\nB &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3$ and $B=-4$, and therefore the solution is\n\n$$\ny = 3 e ^ {2 x} - 4 x e ^ {2 x}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186152001 1074 "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root $\\lambda = 2,$ so we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=3$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A{e}^{2(0)}+B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 3 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y' (0) = 2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny'  = 2 A e ^ {2 x} +  B e ^ { 2 x} + 2 B x e ^{ 2 x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2A{e}^{2(0)}+B{e}^{2(0)}+2B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 2 & =2A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 2 & =2A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3=A \\\\ 2=2A+B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=3$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =6+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3$ and $B=-4,$ and therefore the solution is \n\n$$\ny = 3 e ^ { 2 x } - 4 x e ^ { 2 x}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186152002 1071 "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^x$ are solutions to the equation $y''+y'-2y=0$, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$\n2. $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$\n3. $y(x) = e^{-2x} +e^{x}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186152002 1071 "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^x$ are solutions to the equation $y''+y'-2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x} +e^{x}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186152002 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous differential equation, then\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_1(x) + y_2(x)\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n- The function $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n- The function $y(x) = e^{-2x} +e^{x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186152002 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous differential equation, then $y(x) = y_1(x) + y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) =  e^{-2x} +e^{x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186152003 1071 "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 4y = 0$, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$\n2. $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$\n3. $y(x) = -e^{2x}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186152003 1071 "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 4y = 0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = -e^{2x}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186152003 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the function $y_1(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then\n\n$$\ny(x) = c y_1(x)\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n- The function $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=3$.\n- The function $y(x) = -e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186152003 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the function $y_1(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x) = c y_1(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=3.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = -e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186152011 1071 "![](images/11/1191de029d927394dcc587f9c432a2d7cf4cec136e6d6ebbe62799de7b4f9b3e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,1] \\times [0,1]$, and\n$\\iint\\limits_D \\mid x-y \\mid \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A$,\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)$?\n\n1. ${\\begin{vmatrix}x - y &,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D; 0, \\mid (x, y) ∈ D∖R$\n2. ${0, \\mid (x, y) ∈ R∖D;\\begin{vmatrix}x - y &,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D$\n3. ${\\begin{vmatrix}x - y &,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D; 0, \\mid (x, y) ∉ D$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186152011 1071 "![](images/11/1191de029d927394dcc587f9c432a2d7cf4cec136e6d6ebbe62799de7b4f9b3e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,1] \\times [0,1],$ and\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D |x-y| \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}|x-y|, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ |x-y|, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}|x-y|, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186152011 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R$:\n\n![](images/d9/d9ca0bfcfde5839033279cb09f8ccece7c59a5b8f8b24eaae3697fd5358dc6b3.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely.\n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\mid x-y \\mid$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mid x-y \\mid \\: \\text{