ables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(-1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^2+y^2=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = -2\\cos\\theta.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\pi,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/76/769c13bea2dfc91aa3a507df1b7a763ae0da7591e0d6ba5face12268efb88240.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: -\\sec\\theta \\leq r \\leq -2\\cos\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186157108 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(-1,0)$.\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{x = r\\cos θ; y = r\\sin θ\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^2+y^2=1$, we obtain\n$(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2}|= 1; (r\\cos θ + 1)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2}|= 1; r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ + 2r\\cos θ + 1 + r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ|= 1; r^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) + 2r\\cos θ|= 0; r^{2} + 2r\\cos θ|= 0; r(r + 2\\cos θ)|= 0$.\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = -2\\cos\\theta$.\n- For $x=-1$, we get\n$x|=-1; r\\cos θ|=-1; r|=-\\frac{1}{\\cos θ}; r|=-\\sec θ$.\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta$. Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1)$. So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand $\\theta=\\pi$, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/76/769c13bea2dfc91aa3a507df1b7a763ae0da7591e0d6ba5face12268efb88240.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: -\\sec\\theta \\leq r \\leq -2\\cos\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)xydA &= ∬_(Δ)(r\\cos θ \\cdot r\\sin θ)rdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{3π/4}^{π}\\int_{-\\sec θ}^{-2\\cos θ}r^{3}\\cos θ\\sin θdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{3π/4}^{π}\\cos θ\\sin θ\\int_{-\\sec θ}^{-2\\cos θ}r^{3}drdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186171818 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{1-x}{x+2} < 0$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186171818 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{1-x}{x+2} < 0.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186171818 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1-x}{x+2}\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\lt 0$.\n\nThe solution to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nis $x = 1$. Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x = -2$. The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad (-2,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-2\\right)$ | $\\left(-2,1\\right)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1-x$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) < 0$, we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty,-2)$ and $(1,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty,-2) \\cup (1,\\infty)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] 