$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186175797 1074 "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-32t) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t) <    0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3}-32 & <0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3} & <32 \\\\ {t}^{3} & <8 \\\\ t & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in\\left[ 0, 2\\right).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186175798 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^5 e^x \\, \\textrm d x$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186175798 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^5 e^x \\, \\textrm d x.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186175798 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{5}e^{x}dx &= \\left.e^{x}\\right|_{0}^{5} \\\\\n&= e^{5} - e^{0} \\\\\n&= e^{5} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186175798 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={e}^{x}{|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & ={e}^{5}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{5}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186175804 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t) = 6t^2 +4t$. At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=-20$. What is the position of the particle at time $t=4$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186175804 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t) = 6t^2 +4t.$ At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=-20.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=4?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186175804 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=4$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in[2,4]$. Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(4) = x(2) + \\int_2^4 v(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(4) &= -20 + \\int_{2}^{4}(6t^{2} + 4t)dt \\\\\n&=-20 + [2t^{3} + 2t^{2}]_{2}^{4} \\\\\n&=-20 + ([2(4)^{3} + 2(4)^{2}] - [2(2)^{3} + 2(2)^{2}]) \\\\\n&=-20 + ([128 + 32] - [16 + 8]) \\\\\n&=-20 + (160 - 24) \\\\\n&=-20 + 136 \\\\\n&= 116\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=116$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186175804 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=4$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in[2,4].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(4) = x(2) + \\int_2^4 v(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(4) & =-20+{\\int }_{2}^{4}(6{t}^{2}+4t)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =-20+{[2{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =-20+([2(4)^{3}+2(4)^{2}]-[2(2)^{3}+2(2)^{2}]) \\\\  & =-20+([128+32]-[16+8]) \\\\  & =-20+(160-24) \\\\  & =-20+136 \\\\  & =116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=116.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186175805 1071 "![](images/90/900988cb8ec54abcafc256a34c685829d2f5f00cdca373ad84dfa0ef70cc3957.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=-1$. What is the position of the particle at time $t=8$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186175805 1071 "![](images/90/900988cb8ec54abcafc256a34c685829d2f5f00cdca373ad84dfa0ef70cc3957.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=-1.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=8?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186175805 1074 "The position of the particle at $t = 8$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,8]$. Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(8) = x(2)+ \\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=-1$, and we can compute the integral $\\displaystyle\\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm dt$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=8$. Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm dt = 6\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(8) = -1+ 6 = 5\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=8$ is $x=5$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186175805 1074 "The position of the particle at $t = 8$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,8].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(8) = x(2)+ \\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=-1$, and we can compute the integral $\\displaystyle\\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm dt$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=8.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm dt = 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(8) = -1+ 6 = 5\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=8$ is $x=5.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186175807 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2$, given by the function $a(t) =2t-6$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=1\\,\\textrm s$, determine the moments in time when the particle is stationary." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186175807 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) =2t-6$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=1\\,\\textrm s$, determine the moments in time when the particle is stationary." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186175807 1074 "The particle is stationary when\n\n$$\nv=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\inta(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int(2t - 6)dt \\\\\n&= t^{2} - 6t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) =4$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= (1)^{2} - 6(1) + C \\\\\n4 &= -5 + C \\\\\nC &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t) = t^2-6t + 9\n$$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when\n\n$$\nv=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} - 6t + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(t - 3)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nt &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when\n\n$$\nt=3 \\,\\textrm s\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186175807 1074 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-6)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-6t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) =4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =(1)^{2}-6(1)+C \\\\ 4 & =-5+C \\\\ C & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = t^2-6t + 9.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-6t+9 & =0 \\\\ (t-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=3 \\,\\textrm s.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186175808 1071 "Given that the power series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n (x-2)^n$ converges at  $x=6,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=0$\n\n- The series converges at $x=7$\n\n- The series converges at $x=-2$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186175808 1071 "Given that the power series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n (x-2)^n$ converges at $x=6$, which of the following **must** be true?\n\n1. The series converges at $x=0$\n2. The series converges at $x=7$\n3. The series converges at $x=-2$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186175808 1074 "The center of the expansion is $x=2$, and since the series is convergent for $x=6$, this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $4$, i.e.,\n\n$$\nR \\geq 4\n$$\n\nSo, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-2,6]$. However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-2,6]$.\n\n![](images/45/45ee68ed72cb41c60a72a35fe73ae3f4d868e03fd708675fdbdbbb44fea02e80.png)\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $0\\in (-2,6]$, the series is convergent at $x=0$.\n- Statement II is false. Since $7\\notin (-2,6]$, we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=7$.\n- Statement III is false. Since $-2\\notin (-2,6]$, we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-2$.\n\nTherefore, only statement I must be true." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186175808 1074 "The center of the expansion is $x=2,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=6,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $4,$ i.e., $R \\geq 4 .$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-2,6].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-2,6].$\n\n![](images/45/45ee68ed72cb41c60a72a35fe73ae3f4d868e03fd708675fdbdbbb44fea02e80.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $0\\in (-2,6],$ the series is convergent at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $7\\notin (-2,6],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=7.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $-2\\notin (-2,6],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, only statement I must be true." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186175809 1071 "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with a particular virus  follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} =  4P - 0.000\\,01  P^2,  \\qquad P(0) = 5\\,000,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus outbreak began. For which value of $P$ is the number of people infected with the virus growing at the fastest possible rate?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186175809 1071 "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with a particular virus follows the logistic growth model\n\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4P - 0.000\\,01 P^2, \\qquad P(0) = 5\\,000$,\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus outbreak began. For which value of $P$ is the number of people infected with the virus growing at the fastest possible rate?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186175809 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\nP' = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dP}{dt} &= 4P - 0.00001P^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.00001P(400000 - P)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that\n\n$$\na = 400\\,000\n$$\n\nHence, the carrying capacity is $400\\,000$, and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a = \\dfrac12\\cdot 400\\,000 = 200\\,000\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186175809 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\nP' = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P = \\dfrac12a.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =4P-0.000\\,01{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,01P(400\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 400\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $400\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a = \\dfrac12\\cdot 400\\,000 = 200\\,000.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186179527 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- $P_1 = \\left\\{x_0, x_1, \\ldots, x_m\\right\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[4, 6]$\n\n- $P_2 = \\left\\{y_0, y_1, \\ldots, y_n\\right\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[2, 6]$\n\nCalculate  $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (x_i + x_{i-1})\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j,$ where $\\Delta x_i = x_i - x_{i-1}$ and $\\Delta y_j = y_j - y_{j-1}.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186179527 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- $P_1 = \\left\\{x_0, x_1, \\ldots, x_m\\right\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[4, 6]$\n- $P_2 = \\left\\{y_0, y_1, \\ldots, y_n\\right\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[2, 6]$\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (x_i + x_{i-1})\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j$, where $\\Delta x_i = x_i - x_{i-1}$ and $\\Delta y_j = y_j - y_{j-1}$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186179527 1074 "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m (x_i + x_{i-1})\\Delta x_i \\cdot \\sum_{j=1}^n \\Delta y_j\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{j=1}^n\\Delta y_j\n$$\n\nis just the length of the interval $[2, 6]$. So, we have\n\n$$\n∑_(j = 1)^(n)Δy_{j} = 6 - 2 = 4\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $i$, we can evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(m)(x_{i} + x_{i - 1})Δx_{i} &= (x_{1} + x_{0})Δx_{1} + (x_{2} + x_{1})Δx_{2} + ⋯ + (x_{m} + x_{m - 1})Δx_{n} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{0})(x_{1} - x_{0}) + (x_{2} + x_{1})(x_{2} - x_{1}) + (x_{m} + x_{m - 1})(x_{m} - x_{m - 1}) \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} - x_{0}^{2}) + (x_{2}^{2} - x_{1}^{2}) + ⋯ + (x_{m}^{2} - x_{m - 1}^{2}) \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} - x_{0}^{2}) + (x_{2}^{2} - x_{1}^{2}) + ⋯ + (x_{m}^{2} - x_{m - 1}^{2}) \\\\\n&=-x_{0}^{2} + x_{m}^{2} \\\\\n&= x_{m}^{2} - x_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 6^{2} - 4^{2} \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m (x_i + x_{i-1})\\Delta x_i \\cdot \\sum_{j=1}^n\\Delta y_j = 20\\cdot 4 = 80\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186179527 1074 "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m (x_i + x_{i-1})\\Delta x_i \\cdot \\sum_{j=1}^n \\Delta y_j.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{j=1}^n\\Delta y_j$ is just the length of the interval $[2, 6].$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=6-2=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $i,$ we can evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{0})Δ{x}_{1}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{1})Δ{x}_{2}+⋯+({x}_{m}+{x}_{m-1})Δ{x}_{m} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}\\,+\\,{x}_{0})({x}_{1}\\,-\\,{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}\\,+\\,{x}_{1})({x}_{2}\\,-\\,{x}_{1})+({x}_{m}\\,+\\,{x}_{m-1})({x}_{m}\\,-\\,{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})+({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{m-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}}-{x}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}^{2}+{x}_{m}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={6}^{2}-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m (x_i + x_{i-1})\\Delta x_i \\cdot \\sum_{j=1}^n\\Delta y_j = 20\\cdot 4 = 80.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186179528 1071 "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y$. Further, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nWrite down the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186179528 1071 "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$ Further, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite down the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186179528 1074 "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)M_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/6e/6e58954fccd74eaf67c95df818c4c9050c35b324c5fffe4902a366cb295b8845.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{ij} &= f(x_{i}, y_{j}) = x_{i} + 2y_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i\\leq m\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1\\leq j\\leq n\n$$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i} + 2y_{j})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186179528 1074 "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/6e/6e58954fccd74eaf67c95df818c4c9050c35b324c5fffe4902a366cb295b8845.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j})={x}_{i}+2{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\leq i\\leq m$ and $1\\leq j\\leq n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i}+2{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186179529 1071 "Let $R = [0,1] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nWrite down the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186179529 1071 "Let $R = [0,1] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite down the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186179529 1074 "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)M_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases on $[0,1]$, and\n- $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases on $[0,2]$.\n\n![](images/ce/ce0d90127e1f14654f4b32a933f1eb6e76e054c03f19ca40c3f04116aa3c2c93.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/5b/5b9967c814e46914e0409854de0960e85339adc9b9fd9693677c8e048c342702.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{ij} &= f(x_{i - 1}, y_{j - 1}) = 90 - x_{i - 1}^{2} - y_{j - 1}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i\\leq m\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1\\leq j\\leq n\n$$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(90 - x_{i - 1}^{2} - y_{j - 1}^{2})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186179529 1074 "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases on $[0,1],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 90-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases on $[0,2].$\n\n![](images/ce/ce0d90127e1f14654f4b32a933f1eb6e76e054c03f19ca40c3f04116aa3c2c93.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/5b/5b9967c814e46914e0409854de0960e85339adc9b9fd9693677c8e048c342702.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=90-{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\leq i\\leq m$ and $1\\leq j\\leq n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(90-{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186179530 1071 "![](images/00/001487e4a94988a24d15702a18692e9deb2495a7cc7ec5c9f71b98fea13e0ce8.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $O(0,0)$, and the path $C_2$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1)$.\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = -4$, evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $\\mathbf{F}(x, y) = (2x-3y) \\,\\mathbf{i} + (2y-5x)\\,\\mathbf {j}$ and $C = C_1\\cup C_2$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186179530 1071 "![](images/00/001487e4a94988a24d15702a18692e9deb2495a7cc7ec5c9f71b98fea13e0ce8.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $O(0,0),$ and the path $C_2$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = -4,$ evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x, y) = (2x-3y) \\,\\mathbf{i} + (2y-5x)\\,\\mathbf {j}$ and $C = C_1\\cup C_2.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186179530 1074 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n+\n \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =b+t(c-b) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1.$ Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(2y-5x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3t)\\,i+(2t-5t)\\,j \\\\  & =-t\\,i-3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(-t\\,i-3t\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-t\\cdot 1+(-3t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+[-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4+[-2+0] \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186179530 1074 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B$. It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{2}(t) &= b + t(c - b) \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 0⟩ + t(⟨1, 1⟩ - ⟨0, 0⟩) \\\\\n&= ⟨t, t⟩ \\\\\n&= ti + tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1$. Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r_{2}(t)) &= (2x - 3y)i + (2y - 5x)j \\\\\n&= (2t - 3t)i + (2t - 5t)j \\\\\n&=-ti - 3tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{2}^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r_{2}(t)) \\cdot r_{2}^{′}(t) &= (-ti - 3tj) \\cdot (i + j) \\\\\n&=-t \\cdot 1 + (-3t) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-4t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_(C)F \\cdot dr &= \\int_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr + \\int_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&=-4 + \\int_{0}^{1}F(r_{2}(t)) \\cdot r_{2}^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&=-4 + \\int_{0}^{1}(-4t)dt \\\\\n&=-4 + [- 2t^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-4 + [- 2 + 0] \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186179531 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ along the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186179531 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ along the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186179531 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=3\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\,\\mathbf{i}+3\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =3\\,j+t[(2\\,i+3\\,j)-3\\,j] \\\\  & =3\\,j+t(2\\,i) \\\\  & =2t\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)j \\\\  & =2\\,i \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = e^{x+y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{2t+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t+3,$ $\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\,{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & ={e}^{5}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186179531 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3)$.\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=3\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}=2\\,\\mathbf{i}+3\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + t(b - a) \\\\\n&= 3j + t[(2i + 3j) - 3j] \\\\\n&= 3j + t(2i) \\\\\n&= 2ti + 3j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n0 \\le t \\le 1\n$$\n\nThus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(3)j \\\\\n&= 2i \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2^{2} + 0^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = e^{x+y}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= e^{2t + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_(C)e^{x + y}ds &= \\int_{0}^{1}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}e^{2t + 3} \\cdot 2 \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= 2\\int_{0}^{1}e^{2t + 3}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=2t+3\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\int_{0}^{1}e^{2t + 3}dt &= \\int_{3}^{5}e^{u}du \\\\\n&= \\left.e^{u}\\right|_{3}^{5} \\\\\n&= e^{5} - e^{3} \\\\\n&= e^{3}(e^{2} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186179532 1071 "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = 2x+y^2$ along the quarter-circle $x^2+y^2=4$, traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0)$ in the second quadrant." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186179532 1071 "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = 2x+y^2$ along the qua