389 17592186045585 true] [17592186184087 1071 "Consider the geometric sequence\n$a_n =\\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\right)^{n}, \\qquad n\\geq 1$\n\nwhere $x$ is a positive real number. For which values of $x$ is the sequence convergent?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186184087 1071 "Consider the geometric sequence \n\n$$\na_n =\\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\right)^{n}, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is a positive real number. For which values of $x$ is the sequence convergent?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186184087 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r$. Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n- if $\\mid r \\mid > 1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n- if $r = -1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{16}{x^2}\n$$\n\nSince $x^2$ is positive, $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is\n\n$$\n0 \\lt r\\leq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0 \\lt\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\leq 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x^2 \\geq 16\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx \\geq 4\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186184087 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <                      1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r = \\dfrac{16}{x^2}.$ \nSince $x^2$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0 \\lt r\\leq 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0 \\lt\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\leq 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x^2 \\geq 16.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx \\geq 4.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186184089 1071 "Write the equation $2x^2 - 6x = 1$ in the form $(x+p)^2 = q$, and hence solve the equation." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186184089 1071 "Write the equation $2x^2 - 6x = 1$ in the form $(x+p)^2 = q,$ and hence solve the equation." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186184089 1074 "First, we divide out by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 6x &= 1 \\\\\n2(x^{2} - 3x) &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} - 3x &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot x &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 2 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}) \\cdot x + (\\frac{3}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{3}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{3}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{3}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} - \\frac{9}{4} &= \\frac{2}{4} \\\\\n(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{11}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\np=-\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nq=\\dfrac{11}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{(x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{4}} \\\\\n\\mid x - \\frac{3}{2} \\mid &= \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\\nx - \\frac{3}{2} &= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3}{2} ± \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3 ± \\sqrt{11}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186184089 1074 "First, we divide out by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-6x & =1 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-3x) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-\\dfrac32$ and $q=\\dfrac{11}{4}.$ Now, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{4}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{11}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186184090 1071 "Write the equation $-2x^2+2x+1 = 0$ in the form $2(x+p)^2=q$, and hence solve the equation." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186184090 1071 "Write the equation $-2x^2+2x+1 = 0$ in the form $2(x+p)^2=q,$ and hence solve the equation." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186184090 1074 "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-1) \\cdot (-2x^{2} + 2x + 1) &= (-1) \\cdot 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 2x - 1 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 2x - 1 &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 2x &= 1 \\\\\n2(x^{2} - x) &= 1 \\\\\n2[x^{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x + (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{1}{2})^{2}] &= 1 \\\\\n2[(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2} - \\frac{1}{4}] &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2[(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2} - \\frac{1}{4}] &= 1 \\\\\n2(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n2(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\np=-\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nq=\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2} &= \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\\n\\mid x - \\frac{1}{2} \\mid &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\nx - \\frac{1}{2} &= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2} ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186184090 1074 "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-2{x}^{2}+2x+1) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x & =1 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-x) & =1 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ 2[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =1 \\\\ 2{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\dfrac12$ and $q=\\dfrac32.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186184091 1071 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$f(x)=1 + 4(x-2) - 12(x-2)^2$.\n\nWhat is the value of $f''(2)$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186184091 1071 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)=1 + 4(x-2) - 12(x-2)^2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $f''(2)?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186184091 1074 "This is an expansion about the point $x=2$, so the coefficient of the second-degree term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2)\n$$\n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-12$, so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2) = -12\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f''(2) = -24\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186184091 1074 "This is an expansion about the point $x=2,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2).$ In the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-12,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 f''(2) = -12\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f''(2) = -24.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186184092 1071 "![](images/8b/8b630e44d49440844d668f395dd5d85676e1824f283edb86194a88a62c78b3f7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y = f(x)$. Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$?\n\n1. $-1+2(x+1)-3(x+1)^2$\n2. $-1+4(x+1)+(x+1)^2$\n3. $-1-5(x+1)-2(x+1)^2$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186184092 1071 "![](images/8b/8b630e44d49440844d668f395dd5d85676e1824f283edb86194a88a62c78b3f7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y = f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1?$\n\n- $-1+2(x+1)-3(x+1)^2$\n\n- $-1+4(x+1)+(x+1)^2$\n\n- $-1-5(x+1)-2(x+1)^2$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186184092 1074 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\nP_2(x)=f(-1)+f'(-1)(x+1)+\\dfrac{f''(-1)}{2}(x+1)^2\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-1) <0$. Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n- $f'(-1) >0$, because the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n- $f''(-1) <0$, because the graph is concave down at $x=-1$. Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nOnly option I meets all of the above criteria." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186184092 1074 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\nP_2(x)=f(-1)+f'(-1)(x+1)+\\dfrac{f''(-1)}{2}(x+1)^2.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-1)  <0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- $f'(-1) >0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- $f''(-1) <0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nOnly option I meets all of the above criteria." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186188211 1071 "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ for the polar curve $r=5$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186188211 1071 "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ for the polar curve $r=5.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186188211 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta.$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=5,$ we get\n\n$$\nx =5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = -5\\sin\\theta, \\qquad y'(\\theta) = 5\\cos\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{ 5\\cos\\theta }{  -5\\sin\\theta} =-\\cot\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186188211 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} = (\\frac{d}{dθ}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dθ}))\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta$.\n\nUsing the fact that $r=5$, we get\n\n$$\nx =5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nThen, to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the rule\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(θ) &= -5\\sin θ, \\\\\ny^{′}(θ) &= 5\\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{5\\cos θ}{-5\\sin θ} \\\\\n&=-\\cot θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dθ}(-\\cot θ))/(-5\\sin θ) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\csc^{2} θ}{\\sin θ} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\csc^{2} θ \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sin θ} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\csc^{2} θ \\cdot \\csc θ \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\csc^{3} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186198236 1071 "Given that $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 10t^4 ,\\, 6\\ln t\\rangle,$ what is $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)?$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186198236 1071 "Given that $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 10t^4,\\, 6\\ln t\\rangle$, what is $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186198236 1074 "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t) = 10t^4$, we have\n$x'(t) = 40t^3$,\nand therefore\n$x'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = 40\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^3 = 5$.\n- For the $y$-component $y(t) = 6 \\ln t$, we have\n$y^{′}(t) = \\frac{6}{t}$,\nand therefore\n$y'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{6}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}=12$.\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) &= ⟨x^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}), y^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5, 12⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186198236 1074 "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t) = 10t^4,$ we have\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 40t^3,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nx'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = 40\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^3 = 5.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t) = 6 \\ln t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6}{t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\ny'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{6}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}=12.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨{x}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}),\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186198237 1071 "Given that $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t^6,\\, t^5\\rangle$, Calculate $\\mathbf r''(t)$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186198237 1071 "Given that $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t^6,\\, t^5\\rangle,$ Calculate $\\mathbf r''(t).$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186198237 1074 "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t) = t^6$, we have\n$x^{′}(t)|= 6t^{5}; x^{″}(t)|= 30t^{4}$.\n- For $y(t) = t^5$, we have\n$y^{′}(t)|= 5t^{4}; y^{″}(t)|= 20t^{3}$.\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨x^{″}(t), y^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨30t^{4}, 20t^{3}⟩ \\\\\n&= 10⟨3t^{4}, 2t^{3}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186198237 1074 "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t) = t^6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =6{t}^{5} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =30{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t) = t^5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =5{t}^{4} \\\\ {y}^{″}(t) & =20{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨{x}^{″}(t),\\,{y}^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨30{t}^{4},\\,20{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =10⟨3{t}^{4},\\,2{t}^{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186198240 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186198240 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as \n\n![](images/4a/4a20bc6cf78708f70fb1a696b9dc5971feef974df6429fa4169f82881c6d22e0.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186198240 1074 "Given a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $\\mathbf T$, we need to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = 0\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nx(u,v) = u^2-v^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny(u,v) = u+v\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= 2u & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|=-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= 1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|= 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -2v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2(u + v)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} =0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= 0 \\\\\n2(u + v) &= 0 \\\\\nu &= -v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $T$ consist of those points that lie on the line $u=-v$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186198240 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/62/62dec67282c5bc2707377cb976412a48ca3c41ffeed6aa74ef0d5b1ef8d6e380.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $\\mathbf T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = u^2-v^2$ and $y(u,v) = u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ \\,\\,1\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} =0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2(u+v) & =0 \\\\ u & =-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $T$ consist of those points that lie on the line $u=-v$  in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186221213 1071 "Given that $\\mathcal L\\left\\{t^n\\right\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$, find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = (t - 5)^2$ for $s > 0$." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186221213 1071 "Given that $\\mathcal L\\left\\{t^n\\right\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = (t - 5)^2$ for $s > 0.$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186221213 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nFirst, we expand:\n\n$$\nf(t) = (t - 5)^2= t^2 - 10t + 25\n$$\n\nNow, taking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= L{t^{2} - 10t + 25} \\\\\n&= L{t^{2}} + L{-10t} + L{25} \\\\\n&= L{t^{2}} - 10L{t} + 25L{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{t^n\\right\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{t^{2}} &= \\frac{2}{s^{3}}, s > 0 \\\\\nL{t} &= \\frac{1}{s^{2}}, s > 0 \\\\\nL{1} &= \\frac{1}{s}, s > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= L{t^{2}} - 10L{t} + 25L{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s^{3}} - 10 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s^{2}} + 25 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s^{3}} - \\frac{10}{s^{2}} + \\frac{25}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - 10s + 25s^{2}}{s^{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25s^{2} - 10s + 2}{s^{3}}, s > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186221213 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nFirst, we expand:\n\n$$\nf(t) = (t - 5)^2= t^2 - 10t + 25\n$$\n\nNow, taking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{t}^{2}-10t+25} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+L{-10t}+L{25} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}-10L{t}+25L{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\mathcal L\\left\\{t^n\\right\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}.$ Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{t}^{2}}-10L{t}+25L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-10\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+25\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{25}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-10s+25{s}^{2}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{s}^{2}-10s+2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186228957 1071 "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L(y_1) = \\tan^2{x}$ and $L(y_2) =2 \\sec^2{x}$ for some functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. What is $L(2y_1 -y_2)$?\n\n*Hint: Use $\\sec^2\\theta = 1+\\tan^2\\theta$.*" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186228957 1071 "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L(y_1) = \\tan^2{x}$ and $L(y_2) =2 \\sec^2{x}$ for some functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ What is $L(2y_1 -y_2)?$\n\n*Hint: Use $\\sec^2\\theta = 1+\\tan^2\\theta.$*" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186228957 1074 "For any linear differential operator $L$, we have\n\n$$\nL(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aL(y_1(x)) + bL(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(2y_{1} - y_{2}) &= 2L(y_{1}) - L(y_{2}) \\\\\n&= 2\\tan^{2} x - 2\\sec^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identity\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nwe can simplify this even further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(2y_{1} - y_{2}) &= 2\\tan^{2} x - 2(1 + \\tan^{2} x) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL(2y_1 -y_2)=-2\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186228957 1074 "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have\n\n$$\nL(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aL(y_1(x)) + bL(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{tan}^{2}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identity $1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x,$ we can simplify this even further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2{tan}^{2}⁡x-2(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nL(2y_1 -y_2)=-2.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186228958 1071 "Show that the differential operator\n\n$$\nD(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} + \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nis nonlinear." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186228958 1071 "Show that the differential operator\n\n$D(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} + \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$\n\nis nonlinear." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186228958 1074 "For any linear differential operator $L$, we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(ay_1 + by_2) = aL(y_1) + bL(y_2)\n$$\n\nfor functions $y_1(x), y_2(x)$, and where $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(ay_{1} + by_{2}) &= (ay_{1} + by_{2})\\frac{d^{2}}{dx^{2}}(ay_{1} + by_{2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(ay_{1} + by_{2}) \\\\\n&= (ay_{1} + by_{2})(a\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + b\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}}) + a\\frac{dy_{1}}{dx} + b\\frac{dy_{2}}{dx} \\\\\n&= + a^{2}y_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + aby_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + aby_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + b^{2}y_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} \\\\\n&= + a\\frac{dy_{1}}{dx} + b\\frac{dy_{2}}{dx} \\\\\n&= a^{2}y_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + b^{2}y_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + a\\frac{dy_{1}}{dx} + b\\frac{dy_{2}}{dx} \\\\\n&= + ab(y_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + y_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\naD(y_{1}) + bD(y_{2}) &= a[y_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + \\frac{dy_{1}}{dx}] + b[y_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + \\frac{dy_{2}}{dx}] \\\\\n&= ay_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + by_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + a\\frac{dy_{1}}{dx} + b\\frac{dy_{2}}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\nD(ay_1+by_2) \\neq aD(y_1) + bD(y_2)\n$$\n\nthe operator $D$ is nonlinear." 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186228958 1074 "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(ay_1 + by_2) = aL(y_1) + bL(y_2)\n$$\n\nfor functions $y_1(x), y_2(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =+{a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & +a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & +ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(ay_1+by_2)  \\neq aD(y_1) + bD(y_2),$ the operator $D$ is nonlinear." 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186228959 1071 "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+y=\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x\n= -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186228959 1071 "Consider the following differential equation\n$y''+y=\\tan{x}$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x = -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186228959 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}(x)y_{1}(x) + u_{2}(x)y_{2}(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = f(x); u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x, \\qquad u_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\tan x\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x, \\qquad y_2(x)=\\sin x\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= y_{1}(x)y_{2}^{′}(x) - y_{1}^{′}(x)y_{2}(x) \\\\\n&= (\\cos x)(\\sin x)^{′} - (\\cos x)^{′}(\\sin x) \\\\\n&= (\\cos x)(\\cos x) - (-\\sin x)(\\sin x) \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} x + \\sin^{2} x \\\\\n&= 1 \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}(x) &= -\\int(f(x)y_{2}(x))/(W(y_{1}, y_{2}))dx \\\\\n&=-\\int\\tan x\\sin xdx \\\\\n&= \\sin x - \\ln \\mid \\tan x + \\sec x \\mid \\\\\nu_{2}(x) &= \\int(f(x)y_{1}(x))/(W(y_{1}, y_{2}))dx \\\\\n&= \\int\\tan x\\cos xdx \\\\\n&= \\int\\sin xdx \\\\\n&=-\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}y_{1} + u_{2}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (\\cos x)(\\sin x - \\ln\\begin{vmatrix}\\tan x + \\sec x &) + (-\\cos x)(\\sin x) \\\\ =-\\cos x\\ln & \\tan x + \\sec x\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y_{c}(x) + y_{p}(x) \\\\\n&= A\\cos x + B\\sin x- \\cos x\\ln \\mid \\tan x + \\left.\\sec x\\right|_(⏟)_(g(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186228959 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note t