\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} =\\dfrac{e^{-2s}}{s} +\\dfrac{e^{-6s}}{s}, \\qquad s > 0\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186228961 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise functions $u(t-2)$ and $u(t-6),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2) & ={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 2\\end{aligned} \\\\ u(t-6) & ={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<6 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/6d/6ddb2983dec1f3221778a336bb6ef3cce3138476b67d64334ab6660184a79f95.png)\n\nSo, for our Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{6}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second integral diverges for $s \\leq 0.$\n\nFor $s > 0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{2}^{6}+2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-6s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}(0-{e}^{-6s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-6s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} =\\dfrac{e^{-2s}}{s} +\\dfrac{e^{-6s}}{s}, \n\\qquad \ns > 0.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186231410 1071 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 25y =2\\sin^2{pt}$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186231410 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 25y =2\\sin^2{pt},\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true] [17592186231410 1074 "First, we find the general solution of the differential equation. The complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 25\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 5\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{5 t} + B\\sin{5 t}\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $2 \\sin^2 pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega = 5$. Since\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2 \\sin^2 pt = 1 - \\cos 2pt\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p$. Therefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when\n\n$$\n2p = 5 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad p = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 false] [17592186231410 1074 "First, we find the general solution of the differential equation. The complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 25\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 5,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{5 t} + B\\sin{5 t}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $2 \\sin^2 pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega = 5.$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2 \\sin^2 pt = 1 - \\cos 2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$ Therefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when\n\n$$\n2p = 5 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad p = \\dfrac{5}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533389 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"e-5829" 17592186133631 "e-5835" 17592186133632 "e-5839" 17592186128970 "e-5840" 17592186128971 "e-5841" 17592186128972 "e-5847" 17592186087584 "e-5849" 17592186087585 "e-5851" 17592186087586 "e-5859" 17592186171818 "e-5861" 17592186171819 "e-5884" 17592186119670 "e-5885" 17592186100747 "e-5887" 17592186083050 "e-5888" 17592186128978 "e-5891" 17592186105634 "e-5893" 17592186124366 "e-5894" 17592186124367 "e-5897" 17592186128979 "e-5901" 17592186087590 "e-5902" 17592186128980 "e-5903" 17592186128981 "e-5904" 17592186128982 "e-5905" 17592186128983 "e-5906" 17592186128984 "e-5907" 17592186133634 "e-5908" 17592186133635 "e-5909" 17592186133636 "e-5910" 17592186133637 "e-5913" 17592186133638 "e-5917" 17592186147347 "e-5920" 17592186147348 "e-5921" 17592186147349 "e-5922" 17592186184080 "e-5923" 17592186184081 "e-5924" 17592186068625 "e-5926" 17592186068626 "e-595" 17592186083060 "e-596" 17592186083061 "e-5963" 17592186133640 "e-5965" 17592186128985 "e-5966" 17592186128986 "e-5967" 17592186128987 "e-5968" 17592186124368 "e-597" 17592186083062 "e-5971" 17592186124369 "e-5974" 17592186128988 "e-598" 17592186083063 "e-599" 17592186068627 "e-600" 17592186068628 "e-6017" 17592186064118 "e-602" 17592186092087 "e-6022" 17592186064119 "e-603" 17592186092088 "e-6074" 17592186083071 "e-6075" 17592186083072 "e-6078" 17592186124370 "e-6080" 17592186092089 "e-6087" 17592186124371 "e-609" 1759218619823